rega attia mahmoud parekh dhandibhotla pradhan alugula cevallos cueva athanti abdin e45530 gedefie shimeles motbainor kassanew erg11 gmr diseas nowakowska kurnatowska stray wilczynski abdelaziz bilal omdurman khartoum ctries jidc melhem szeszs mato s1517 denning kneale rautemaa e339 ignjatovic arsic arsenijevic golubovic đenic momcilovic trajkovic ranđelovic ciric otasevic candidosis jof6030113 benhadj menasria ranque tebessa ranđelovic dimitrijevic mijatovic ignjatovic arsic arsenijevic stojanovic radic otasevic s42770 sangare blankson hna seanefu obeng nkrumah awuah ayeh kumi mycol mycmed mucci cuestas cervetto landaburu mujica vulvovaginitis lakshmi santhi guru lavanya leucorrhoea evolut jemds gul ikram lalani peshawar idr s153116 akerele abhulimen okonofua kiasat rezaei matehkolaei zarei mahmoudabadi hamidavi mohamadpour molavi koshajand ahvaz jundishapur e89815 guzel ilkit burgut urunsak ozgunen vaginitis mycopathologia s11046 shawaky shammari mma sewelliam ghazal aaer vaginitis alexandria caramalac birman biotypes 0b013e31806166d7 kalkanci guzel ilkit kustimur vaginitis boric mycol beacon jmm hussen aliyo abbai dedecha bule hora aguin s11908 aboagye waikhom asiamah tettey mbroh osei asafo adjei asmah pecies spectr e0078725 gerginic salovic djuricic jurisic petricevic milicic arsic arsenijevic ceog37738 karakoyun unal sucu bingol unal ilkit mycopathologia s11046 deckx pirotta driel thrush cd009151 cd009151','Abstract Candida is a common and harmless component of the vaginal microbiota, present in approximately 50% of women of reproductive age. In specific conditions like pregnancy, Candida may cause chronic or recurrent infections which significantly impair quality of life. Additionally, due to the possibility of vertical transmission, it may lead to life-threatening infections in newborns. Timely screening, suspicion and identification of the causative agent are critical determinants of patient outcomes. Candida albicans (CA) remains the most prevalent species, but other non- albicans Candida (NAC) species and other non- Candida yeast (NCY) also play an important role in vulvovaginal infections. Due to the lack of precise local epidemiological data, this study aimed to determine the 10-year prevalence of CA, NAC and NCY species (spp.) in symptomatic pregnant women in Serbia using identification Matrix-Assisted Laser Desorption/Ionization Time-of-Flight Mass Spectrometry (MALDI-TOF MS) for species identification. The total of 2,142 cases were examined (2013–2023) and the laboratory positivity was 48.3% (n = 1,035). The prevalence of CA, NAC and NCY were 74%, 23% and 3%, respectively. Biochemical and proteomic identification methods showed 100% concordance for CA, C. krusei ( Pichia kudriavzevii ), C. kefyr ( Kluyveromyces marxianus ), C. lusitaniae ( Clavispora lusitaniae ), and C. zeylanoides ( Pichia norvengensis ). Biochemical misidentification was observed for C. tropicalis (Lodderomyces sp. ) , C. glabrata (Nakaseomyces glabrata) , and Saccharomyces cerevisiae . A global trend highlights the importance of renamed and reclassified yeast species in vaginal infections, supporting the reconsideration of the current disease name VVC in favor of the term vulvovaginal yeast infection (VYI). The prevalence of emerging NAC and NCY species is increasing but remains underestimated. There is a need for new laboratory diagnostic guidelines to enable timely and accurate identification, as this is crucial for guiding appropriate treatment and improving outcomes. Keywords: Serbia, Vulvovaginal yeast infections, Pregnancy, Candida , Identification MALDI-TOF MS, Prevalence Introduction A vaginal yeast infection (VYI), also known as vulvovaginal candidiosis (VVC), is very common during reproductive period, and by definition, it is an infection caused by yeast overgrowth of the estrous-associated vagina and vestibule that can extend beyond the labia minora, labia majora, and perianal region. During pregnancy, more than 30% of women develop this infection, with the potential to affect both the mother and the newborn [ 1 ], to cause adverse perinatal outcomes, including life-threatening invasive diseases [ 2 ], or to increase the risk of preterm birth [ 3 ]. Since the elevated levels of reproductive hormones in pregnancy stimulate the proliferation of yeast it seems to be one of the important factor which leads to infection [ 4 ]. All this is making relevant aspects of the pregnancy important for the diagnostic and for the therapeutic strategy critical to prevent adverse obstetric outcomes, maternal–fetal transmission, and neonatal disease [ 5 ]. This infection is commonly diagnosed clinically, but microbiology confirmation and laboratory procedure accuracy are of great importance, especially if recurrence of disease or pregnancy occurs. High vaginal swab (HVS) have long been considered the reference standard and are used in most investigations either alone or alongside other sampling techniques. This sample type is superior because it provides the most relevant microbiological data while preventing contamination from the skin. Traditionally, HSV samples are used for cultivation and yeasts isolation and identification. But, the quantitative culture results, expressed as yeast quantification done by colony forming units (CFU) determination seems also important, since the yeast species and CFU number may play the key role and guide the proper treatment. Although literature data showed significant species prevalence varies by the region, and historically C. albicans (CA) was the most common species identified in women with the disease, but this species is usually responsible for mild or moderate severity. Epidemiological studies published from 2003 to 2014, concerning the distribution of Candida ( C .) species in women with VVC for CA reported rang from 20.3 to 89%, folowed by C. glabrata (3.4–50.4%), C. tropicalis (0–17.9%), C. parapsilosis (0–10.2%), and C. krusei (0–5.8%) [ 6 ]. Since non- albicans Candida species (NAC) and non- Candida yeasts (NCY), e.g. Saccharomyces cerevisiae , are now considered responsible for complicated forms of these infections, treatment failure and adverse pregnancy outcomes the local data for pregnancy seems important [ 7 ]. Disha and coauthors reported the distribution of different species of Candida isolated from pregnant women across the world. Depending on the population and study design CA reported rang from 25.9 to 87.4%, folowed by C. glabrata (2.2–57.4%), C. krusei (0–21.96%), C. tropicalis (0–21.64%), and C. parapsilosis (0–5.4%) [ 8 ]. Different yeast species have a different pathogenesis background, which is why they are attracting an increasing attention from both clinicians and microbiologists. If an inaccurate identification occur, the symptoms might be result from CA, intend of NCY. In line with this, a new taxonomy of yeasts has recently been adopted, confirming that NAC species (formerly Candida species) have been reclassified into new and distinct genera, e.g. Pihia , Clavispora , Nakaseomyces and Kluyveromyces [ 9 ]. This yeasts reclassification are particularly important for C. glabrata ( Nakaseomyces glabrata ) and C. krusei ( Pichia kudriavzevii ), which are associated with an increase in the rate of more severe infections and exhibit antifungal resistance. These species, along with other NAC and NCY species, are intrinsically resistant to azoles, and some are considered potential multidrug-resistant pathogens. This may complicate treatment during pregnancy and accurate yeast identification is important for proper treatment guidelines and recommendations [ 10 ]. Majority of existing data are based on yeast identification done with a combination of phenotypic, morphological and biochemical characteristics and chromogenic media cultivation. Data about global or local prevalence of the value of vaginal NAC and NCY species in pregnancy done by the proteomics-based identification which has been replaced biochemical identification by a more precise Matrix-Assisted Laser Desorption/Ionization Time-of-Flight Mass Spectrometry (MALDI-TOF MS) platform are still limited. Recently, proteomics-based identification with newly adopted clinical and mycological criteria now may help the correct fungi identification and determination of effective treatment in critical cases, especially if NCY is suspected [ 11 ]. This newly adopted clinical mycology workflow, which enables more accurate species identification, is particularly important for emerging yeast such as C. krusei = Pichia kudriavzevii and C . glabrata = Nakaseomyces glabrata . Laboratory based prevalence studies using MALDI TOF MS are uncommon, and may be of importance for the more successful implementation of a personalized approach in prevention and treatment of yeast infection during pregnancy [ 12 ]. Therefore, an update of the research on Candida yeast species, but also species other than Candida in vaginal infections during pregnancy is necessary, along with further research and publication of local epidemiological data, tacking into consideration new disease term which is necessarily to switch from VVC to VYI. Since, the prevalence studies of Candida and other yeasts in vulvovaginal infections during pregnancy from Serbia is lacking the primary aim of the study was to investigate the ten-year incidence of VVC in pregnant women in Serbia and to report the prevalence of CA, NAC, and NCY strains identified by application of traditional biochemical methods. The secondary aim was to perform proteomic MALDI-TOF MS method to re-identified our ten-year yeasts collection, and to compare the accuracy of species identification between biochemical and proteomic methods. Materials and Methods Patient Selection and Sample Collection The study was designed as a multi-center prospective study. Patient samples were collected for analysis from several Gynecology and Obstetrics Clinics located in Belgrade and Niš between January 2013 and December 2023. Symptomatic pregnant women with suspected yeast vaginal infection or VVC underwent laboratory investigation, and the single HVS was used for fungal recovery. The Ethical Committee of the University of Niš, Faculty of Medicine, and the Ethical Committee of the University of Belgrade, Faculty of Medicine approved this research. It was conducted according to the ethical guidelines of the Declaration of Helsinki and the ethical policies of the journal, as noted on the journal’s author guidelines page. Written informed consent was obtained from all study participants. Yeast Identification by Morphological and Biochemical Assays Between December 2013 and December 2023 the isolation and yeasts identification were done using standard mycological procedures. HVS was inoculated in two media: Sabouraud dextrose agar (SDA, Liofilchem Diagnostici Roseto degli Abruzzi, Italy) and chromogenic Candida media (Liofilchem/Bacteriology products, Diagnostici Roseto degli Abruzzi, Italy or HiMedia, India), both media incubated at 37 °C up to 7 days. Positive samples were identified by using the germ tube test, the chromogenic media, and by Auxocolor™ (BioRad, Marnes-la-Coquette, France), and CA , NAC and NCY species were reported. The yeast isolates prepared for long term preservation at − 80 °C at the Serbian National Reference Laboratory for Medical Mycology, at Institute of Microbiology and Immunology, Faculty of Medicine, University of Belgrade. A large inoculum of yeast from freshly growth SDA plate is suspended in the cryovials with protective solution (30% glycerol). From each strain the duplicate tube were prepared and storage. Yeast Re-identification by Proteomic MALDI-TOF MS Assay Strain re-identification was done in 2024 by using MALDI-TOF MS Biotyper Sirius one IVD System (Bruker Daltonics, Bremen, Germany). A frozen yeast in cryovials were removed from storage at room temperature, them mixed and a small amount of yeast suspension is then transfer to a liquid SDA media for revival. For proteomic identification samples were prepared using a routine protocol involving the treatment with 1 µL of 70% formic acid and alpha-cyano-4-hydroxycinnamic acid (HCCA) matrix. The identification of the isolates was performed using MBT Compass software, version 4.1.100, in automatic runs provided by flexControl, version 3.4.207.20 (Bruker Daltonics, Bremen, Germany). The MBT Compass Library, Revision H, 3893 species/entries database was used in this research to compare generated mass spectra. Data Presentation and Statistical Analysis The accuracy of species identification for CA , NAC and NCY, as well as the overlap between biochemical and proteomic MALDI-TOF MS methods, was analyzed and expressed as a percentage agreement (%). Kappa statistical analysis and p -value were done to assess the identification accuracy. Data on species identification based on proteomic and revised fungal taxonomy, with new yeast genera and their prevalence, are presented. Results Characteristics of Fungal Species Collected From Pregnant Women in Serbia (2013–2023) From 2013 to 2023 symptomatic pregnant women without previous antifungal treatment were tested. A single sample of HVS (n = 2,142) was examined and a total of 1,035 (48.3%) samples were found to be positive for yeasts. The occurrence of CA, NAC, and NCY species was determined by using morphology and biochemical (standard) identification, and re-identification was done by using the proteomic MALDI-TOF MS method (Table 1 A). Morphological and biochemical (standard) identification revealed the prevalence of yeasts from the genera Candida and Saccharomyces , as presented in Table 1 A. CA, NAC and NCY were present in 73.20%, 23.70%, and 3.10% of samples, respectively. Table 1. Candida albicans (CA) , non -albicans Candida (NAC) and non -Canida yeast (NCY) species (n = 1,035) optimal accuracy of identification and overlap between standard and MALDI-TOF MS methods (n = 917), including new taxonomy (part A ); Candida spp. and Saccharomyces spp. (n = 113) incomplete accuracy (part B ); Final proteomic based species identification based on new taxonomy, including the genera Pichia , Clavispora , Nakaseomyces, Loddermyces clade and Kluyveromyces (part C ) Part A Standard identification n = 917 MALDI-TOF MS re-identification n = 917 Overlap Kappa p -value C. albicans n = 758 C. albicans n = 758 100% 1.0 < 0.001 C. krusei n = 112 Pichia kudriavzevii n = 112 100% 1.0 < 0.001 C. kefyr n = 23 Kluyveromyces marxianus n = 23 100% 1.0 < 0.001 C. zeylanoides n = 17 Pichia norvengensis n = 17 100% 1.0 < 0.001 C. lusitaniae n = 7 Clavispora lusitaniae n = 7 100% 1.0 < 0.001 Part B Standard identification n = 113 MALDI-TOF MS re-identification n = 113 Overlap Kappa p value C. tropicalis n = 53 C. tropicalis n = 20 37.7% 0.786 < 0.001 Clavispora lusitaniae n = 33 C. glabrata n = 28 Nakaseomyces glabrata C. Glabrata n = 15 53.4% 0.913 < 0.001 Kluyveromyces marxianus n = 10 Pichia kudriavzevii n = 3 Saccharomyces spp. n = 32 * S. cerevisiae n = 28 87.5% 0.921 < 0.001 Kluyveromyces marxianus n = 4 Part C Standard identification MALDI-TOF MS re-identification C. albicans n = 758 C. albicans n = 758 C. krusei = Pichia kudriavzevii n = 112 C. krusei = Pichia kudriavzevii n = 112 C. kefyr = Kluyveromyces marxianus n = 23 C. kefyr = Kluyveromyces marxianus n = 39 C. zeylanoides = Pichia norvengensis n = 17 C. zeylanoides = Pichia norvengensis n = 17 C. lusitaniae = Clavispora lusitaniae n = 7 C. lusitaniae = Clavispora lusitaniae n = 40 C. tropicalis n = 53 C. tropicalis = Loddermyces clade n = 20 C. glabrata n = 28 C. glabrata = Nakaseomyces glabrata n = 15 Saccharomyces spp. n = 32 S. cerevisiae n = 28 Open in a new tab No determined Candida spp* (n = 5) identified as Pichia kudriavzevii (n = 3) and Kluyveromyces marxianus (n = 2); *At the genus level overlap The predominant NAC species was C. krusei (10.82%) , followed by C. tropicalis (5.12%) , C. glabrata (2.70%) , C. kefir (2.22%) , , C. zeylanoides (1.64%) , , and C. lusitaniae (0.67%). Saccharomyces cerevisiae was a single strain from NCY group. Proteomic-based Identification of Candida and Yeast Species Observed by MALDI-TOF MS Re-identification of 1,035 yeast isolates was performed using MALDI-TOF MS, and the results, based on re-identification and updated taxonomy, are presented in Table 1 C. In this study, the causative agents of VVC or VYI were found to belong to seven yeast genera. In addition to Candida and Saccharomyces , the genera Pichia , Clavispora , Nakaseomyces, Loddermyces and Kluyveromyces were also identified. Indeed, some of these species are well-known causative agents of VVC/VYI, but are now classified differently due to the new taxonomy: C. krusei = Pichia kudriavzevii , C. kefyr = Kluyveromyces marxianus , C. lusitaniae = Clavispora lusitaniae , C. zeylanoides = Pichia norvengensis, and C. glabrata = Nakaseomyces glabrata, and C. tropicalis = Loddermyces clade. Statistical Analysis The 100% overlap between standard and proteomic identification was observed for CA, and some NAC yeasts: C . krusei ( Pichia kudriavzevii ), C. kefir ( Kluyveromyces marxianus ), C. zeylanoides ( Pichia norvengensis ), and C. lusitanie ( Clavispora lusitaniae ). Standard identification showed inaccuracies in the cases of C. tropicalis , C. glabrata , Saccharomyces spp., and Candida spp. The percentage of overlap and the level of significance are presented in Table 1 B. Final yeast species identification using MALDI-TOF MS showed differences in the distribution of Candida species as well as in the prevalence of other yeast genera. Finally, a moderately higher prevalence of C. lusitaniae = Clavispora lusitaniae was observed (n = 40;3.86%), along with lower prevalence of C. tropicalis = Loddermyces clade (n = 20;1.93%), C. glabrata = Nakaseomyces glabrata (n = 15;1.45%), and S. cerevisiae (n = 28;2.70%) (Table 1 C). Discussion This is the first large epidemiological study focusing on VVC/VYI in pregnant women in Serbia. Published data indicated the prevalence of CA, NAC and NCY, yeasts which can cause conditions associated with significant discomfort during pregnancy and may also potentially impact neonatal outcomes, including sepsis [ 13 ]. Recently, a high rate of vertical transmission, nearly 45%, was reported in a non-developed African country, with CA and C. krusei identified as the primary causative agents [ 14 ]. Timely detection and accurate identification of yeast species have the potential of improving patient outcomes and contributing to better understanding of the roles of CA, NAC and NCY species, particularly in complicated cases, recurrent infections, or severe neonatal infections. Severe infections, including sepsis, impose a significant healthcare cost burden, with the problem worsening in low- and middle-income countries, including Serbia. In such cases, it is crucial to implement appropriate antepartum laboratory screening and species identification to enable timely prevention of adverse outcomes. The prevalence of Candida and other yeast species in vulvovaginal infections during pregnancy in Serbia was 48.3%. These relatively high positive rate could be explained by the fact that the study was conducted only in symptomatic women and in the NRMML. Literature data reveal a wide variation in the incidence of VVC/VYI during pregnancy, ranging from 17.2 [ 15 ] to 55.5% [ 16 ], and reaching up to 59.8% in women with diabetes mellitus [ 17 ]. Additionally, the prevalence of CA among all Candida species varied widely, ranging from 16.6 [ 18 ] to 92.3% [ 19 ]. In the review by Gonçalves and colleagues, the lowest incidence rates of laboratory-confirmed VVC/VYI among symptomatic women were reported in Greece (12.1%), Italy (17.7%), and India (19.5%). Conversely, the highest incidence rates were observed in Africa, with 53.7% in Nigeria and 48% in Tunisia, based on studies published between 2003 and 2014 [ 6 ]. Additionally, four studies assessed the prevalence of confirmed infections in asymptomatic women, reporting the lowest incidence in Italy (11.6%), followed by India (15%) and Brazil (14–17%) [ 6 ]. These results agree with the findings showing that African and Asian countries, along with Brazil, have a high incidence of VVC/VYI in examined pregnant women. Species form NAC and NCY are known to contribute to complicated VVC/VYI, which impacts approximately 138 million women worldwide each year, with an annual prevalence of 3,871 cases per 100,000 women. Women aged 25–34 years exhibit the highest prevalence at 9%. Estimates of complicated VVC/VYI vary across countries, ranging from 2,990 per 100,000 women in Timor-Leste, and 3,063 in Niger, to 5.580 cases per 100,000 women in the United Arab Emirates, reflecting demographic variations. Regions such as South and Middle Asia, South and Middle America, North Africa, and the Malaysian islands show the highest estimated number, exceeding 4,000 cases per 100,000 women. Nearly half of all analyzed cases involved patients with recurrent infections, indicating a relatively high prevalence [ 20 ]. Previously published data from Serbia reported yeast prevalence based on biochemical identification methods. The diversity of species involved in VVC/VYI was lower than that observed in the present study, and data on NCY were not reported. Additionally, this study used cluster analysis and identified three distinct sign and symptom clusters of recurrent VVC/VYI: irritation (burning, erythema, itching), uncommon symptoms (odor, edema), and discharge (discharge, whitish discharge) [ 21 ]. Traditionally, S. cerevisiae was recognized as the sole species within the NCY group and has commonly been underestimated. In this study, S. cerevisiae was detected at a low rate (2.70%), based on proteomic identification, consistent with findings reported from China. The authors found the clinical relevance of S. cerevisiae and suggested prevention and treatment strategies tailored to this species, given its distinct pathogenesis and antifungal resistance profiles [ 11 ]. The majority of literature reports standard biochemical assays, primarily Candida chromagar, for yeast identification. The recent introduction of proteomic identification by MALDI-TOF MS has highlighted several previously neglected and rare species. In this work, although statistical significance was not achieved, the data suggested certain advantages of MALDI-TOF MS for yeast identification. With the recent adoption of yeast identification methods, it has been established that the genus Candida is a taxonomically heterogeneous, comprising distantly related species. Several medically important species formerly classified within this genus have now been reassigned to different genera, including Pichia , Clavispora , Nakaseomyces , Loddermyces clade, and Kluyveromyces [ 9 ]. The results demonstrate that standard identification methods consistently agree with MALDI-TOF MS for the majority of species, as shown by a high Kappa value and a very low p-value. This indicates a strong agreement between the two techniques, suggesting that conventional methods may be sufficient for diagnostic purposes in low- and middle-income countries. Thus, in the present study, MALDI TOF MS enabled identification beyond Candida spp., detecting Pichia kudriavzevii and Kluyveromyces marxianus (Table 1 ). Fungal taxonomy must comply with the International Code of Nomenclature for Algae, Fungi, and Plants [ 9 ]. In the present study we incorporated these taxonomic updates by using proteomic MALDI-TOF MS identification instead of traditional biochemical methods. These data showed ideal overlaps for CA, C. krusei (= Pichia kudriavzevii ), C. kefyr (= Kluyveromyces marxianus ), C. zeylanoides (= Pichia norvengensis ), and C. lusitanie (= Clavispora lusitaniae ) (Table 1 ), consistent with findings reported in the literature. However, the identification of C. krusei as Pichia kudriavzevii , C. kefyr as Kluyveromyces marxianus, C. lusitaniae as Clavispora lusitaniae , and C. zeylanoides as Pichia norvengensis (based on MALDI-TOF MS) may cause some confusion. Additionally, some other NAC and NCY strains tend to be misidentified, such as C . tropicalis , C. glabrata , and S. cerevisiae . Therefore, the usage of MALDI-TOF MS has been recommended as one of the most adequate proteomic-based methods for yeast identification in every day practice. Our findings are supported by data from Algeria, and concerns about the global emergence of NAC and NCY have been confirmed in a recent study involving non-pregnant women with VVC/VYI [ 22 , 23 ]. Currently, there are no universally accepted laboratory guidelines for sampling and testing. HVS are predominantly used; however, other sample types, such as cervical swabs [ 18 , 24 – 27 ], endocervical swabs [ 28 , 29 ], rectal swabs [ 30 ], and vaginal self-sampling methods, are occasionally reported in the literature. HVS have long been considered the reference standard and this sample type is superior because it provides the most relevant microbiological data while preventing contamination from the skin. The literature describes alternative methods to cultivation, including immunological techniques based on direct immunofluorescence (DIF) [ 18 ], molecular methods based on DNA amplification (PCR) [ 31 ], fungal genotype-based method [ 32 ], detection of the IGS1 sequence [ 33 ], and DNA-based detection methods [ 34 ]. These methods are rarely used, which makes it difficult to analyze and compare their clinical significance. It is well established that pregnancy, hormone replacement therapy, uncontrolled diabetes mellitus, immunosuppression, antibiotic and glucocorticoid use, genetic predispositions, as well as the use of oral contraceptives, intra-uterine devices, spermicides, condoms and some sexual, hygienic and clothing habits, are host- and behavior-related risk factors for VVC/VYI [ 6 ]. There’s insufficient data on the impact of risk factors such as antibacterial drug use, vaginal microbiota composition, hormonal influences, and genetic factors on the development of VVC/VYI during pregnancy, as well as yeast species distribution. Our results identified seven yeast genera causing vaginal infections in pregnant woman in Serbia, based on new yeast taxonomy: Candida , Saccharomyces , Pichia , Clavispora , Nakaseomyces, Loddermyces clade and Kluyveromyces. The presence of different yeast genera along with an increased number of NAC and NCY species can contribute to treatment failure due to their varying resistance profiles. Data reported differences in prevalence and management of VVC and other yeasts infections in pregnant and non-pregnant women are of great importance, but mostly missing. Traditionally CA remains the main species, though NAC and NCY strains are increasingly noted recently, especially in pregnant women [ 35 ]. Therefore the antifungal management differs between patient groups. The official guidelines recommending topical clotrimazole and/or nystatin, and systemic fluconazole and/or itraconazole. Non-pregnant women can be treated effectively with short-course topical azoles such as clotrimazole or miconazole, or with a single-dose oral azole like fluconazole. Regarding recurrent infections, longer regimens or alternative agents like boric acid or nystatin may be used [ 36 ]. However, in pregnancy, only topical azoles, preferably clotrimazole or miconazole, for at least seven-day-therapy are recommended since oral azoles like fluconazole have been associated with teratogen risk, particularly with high doses or prolonged use [ 37 ]. Timely and accurate diagnosis and appropriate use of antifungal drugs are essential to reduce perinatal complications or recurrent symptoms that affect quality of life [ 29 , 31 , 36 , 38 ]. Practical Implications This data might influence current clinical practice, since the management is often based on clinical presentation rather than laboratory confirmation. A previous study conducted in Serbia reveled that 26.7% of biofilm producing C. glabrata isolates exhibited resistance to fluconazole. Clotrimazole was most effective against CA , C. krusei, C. kefyr and S. cerevisiae , while one-third of C. glabrata isolates showed resistance to it [ 23 ] . In practice, treatment decisions typically follow established guidelines, which recommend antifungal therapy for initial (sporadic) episodes and specify tailored regimens for recurrent infections aimed at maintaining remission. Most guidelines endorse the use of clotrimazole and nystatin for topical treatment, alongside fluconazole or itraconazole for systemic therapy. Existing guidelines for VVC/VYI primarily focus on antifungal treatment, while laboratory-based guidelines for appropriate sampling, diagnostic methods, and species identification remain lacking. Looking ahead, the development of laboratory-based guideline is crucial, as they would enable investigation of NAC and NCY species in VVC/VYI and more precise determination of their roles. Recently, Karakoyun et al. emphasized the need for a diagnostic algorithm to enhance collaboration between clinicians and microbiologists, particularly for complicated vaginal yeast infections during pregnancy, and recommended incorporating MALDI-TOF MS into routine work [ 39 ]. Some authors conclude that challenges also arise from the lack of standardized questionnaires and accurate assessment of symptoms and risk factors for VVC/VYI [ 40 ]. These tools could serve as complementary and essential pillars for successful personalized prevention and laboratory confirmation of VVC/VYI [ 4 , 38 ]. The development of these two essential components has the potential to significantly transform current clinical mycology practice and promote a more personalized approach to VVC/VYI. In conclusion, our local epidemiological data highlight the increased but underestimated prevalence of NAC and NCY yeast species in pregnancy. Globally, still is lacking data for these rare pathogens although they are connected with pure neonatal outcomes. Therefore, during pregnancy is important recognizing the renamed and reclassified yeast species involved in vaginal infections. This may support reconsidering the current terminology, suggesting a change from VVC to VYI Globally, still no data for common or rare yeasts pathogen strongly linked with poor neonatal outcomes, since further research is needed. Acknowledgements This research was funded by The Science Fund of the Republic of Serbia Program Diaspora, Project “Combined Chrom agar Candida spp. and Streptococcus agalactiae test for screening vaginal colonization in pregnant women”—acronym CCA-CSAT-SVCPW, (grant number 6466878); and Program Ideas Project “Prediction, prevention and patients participation in diagnosis of selected fungal infections (FI): An implementation of novel method for obtaining tissue specimens”—acronym FungalCaseFinder, (grant number 7754282). Author Contributions V.A.A., conceptualization; S.O., and Lj.P, methodology; V.G., and M.R., validation; A.J, S.O., and M.R., resources and data curation; V.A.A., and Lj.P, writing original draft preparation and visualization; V.G., project administration; V.A.A., supervision and funding acquisition. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript. Funding This research was funded by The Science Fund of the Republic of Serbia Program Diaspora, Project “Combined Chrom agar Candida spp. and Streptococcus agalactiae test for screening vaginal colonization in pregnant women”—acronym CCA-CSAT-SVCPW, (grant number 6466878); and Program Ideas Project “Prediction, prevention and patients participation in diagnosis of selected fungal infections (FI): An implementation of novel method for obtaining tissue specimens”—acronym FungalCaseFinder, (grant number 7754282). Data Availability Statement All data needed for the study''s reproducibility are given within the text. Declarations Conflicts of i