INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1729,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12440240','Durvalumab versus Physician''s Choice Chemotherapy in Recurrent Ovarian Clear Cell Adenocarcinoma (MOCCA/APGOT-OV2/GCGS-OV3): A Multicenter, Randomized, Phase 2 Trial.','Ngoi NYL, Choi CH, Zhu J, Lim D, Tan TZ, Sun H, Heong V, Ow SGW, Chay WY, Kim HS, Lim YW, Lim SE, Goss G, Goh JC, Kim JW, Friedlander M, Tai BC, Kim K, Tan DSP.','2025','roccc roccc mocca roccc roccc roccc noncomparative roccc mocca roccc roccc roccc multikinase temsirolimus icb roccc icb roccc roccc gy003 fivefold icb roccc mocca apgot ov2 gcgs ov3 roccc roccc nct03405454 roccc icb irae cenduit intolerable intolerable trae iraes traes ov28 roccc foundationonecdx baf250a ventana rrid scr_026335 ge00621 rrid ab_2833074 baf250a hpa005456 rrid ab_1078205 rrid ab_3669002 rrid ab_2936886 rrid ab_2936885 rrid ab_3669003 baf250a malay curtailed curtailed nontarget orrs traes trae traes traes traes amylase hypomagnesemia traes iraes traes hypomagnesemia amylase discoloration traes discoloration traes ov28 s4a s4b s4c s4d foundationonecdx s5a ppp2r1a s5a s5b pik3c2b pik3c2g pik3r1 rptor s5c s5a s5a s5a roccc mocca roccc icb peacocc bruog354 noncomparative roccc mocca icb roccc roccc roccc roccc orrs sintilimab apgot ov7 ov80 roccc traes detriment icb mc38 h1047r mocca mocca mocca malay traes roccc 0201_supplementary_data_s1_suppfs1 0201_supplementary_figure_s1_suppfs1 994kb 0201_supplementary_figure_s2_suppfs2 471kb 0201_supplementary_figure_s3_suppfs3 229kb 0201_supplementary_figure_s4_suppfs4 579kb 0201_supplementary_figure_s5_suppfs5 0201_supplementary_figure_s6_suppfs6 0201_supplementary_table_s1_suppts1 13kb ov28 0201_supplementary_table_s2_suppts2 0201_supplementary_table_s3_suppts3 0201_supplementary_table_s4_suppts4 mocca anzgog gcig mocca nmrc mohiafcat1 csasi21jun pangestu clincancerres aacrjournals ngoi biontech beigene csasi21jun pangestu nonfinancial nonfinancial pmv nonfinancial karyopharm nonfinancial biontech ngoi heong chay goss teoh osann kapp kamura kigawa terakawa kita heong herrington laforets kotantaki garsed kristeleit yaegashi sakuma saga glasspool mcneish westermann nintedanib bibf1120 niccc ov36 a127 dsp rye barrie laframboise fyles roccc takenaka kawabata yanaihara shahin konstantinopoulos farley uyar gershenson cabozantinib gy001 farley yonemori bonebrake temsirolimus temsirolimus howitt sholl rodig ritterhouse oncoimmunology e1277308 oyarvide longacre fraune rosebrock magg makrypidi fraune kluth stoler gadducci multinu cosio carinelli ghioni aletti murali kobel mutability unleashed anglesio kulbe rischin lemech yanaihara anglesio ochiai hirata lamano lamano didomenico choy veliceasa e000285 kiyohara uhara iizuka uehara reinvigoration matulonis shapira frommer santin lisyanskaya pignata zamarin sill lankes ahrenfeldt frequentist kristeleit gourley 521mo ccgc peacocc s783 dizon machan hadfield bruog lba5500 othus ct162 swog s1609 ct162 zielinski 998mo noncolorectal s679 sintilimab inova ngoi thian 788p ccgc gcgs ov4 apgot ov3 s589 dsp coquard nieuwenhuysen dostarlimab nonplatinum apgot ov7 ov80 tps5627 abosy e003402 0201_supplementary_data_s1_suppfs1 0201_supplementary_figure_s1_suppfs1 994kb 0201_supplementary_figure_s2_suppfs2 471kb 0201_supplementary_figure_s3_suppfs3 229kb 0201_supplementary_figure_s4_suppfs4 579kb 0201_supplementary_figure_s5_suppfs5 0201_supplementary_figure_s6_suppfs6 0201_supplementary_table_s1_suppts1 13kb ov28 0201_supplementary_table_s2_suppts2 0201_supplementary_table_s3_suppts3 0201_supplementary_table_s4_suppts4','Abstract Purpose: The optimal treatment of recurrent ovarian clear cell carcinoma (rOCCC) remains unknown. This is the first randomized trial to compare durvalumab with chemotherapy in rOCCC. Patients and Methods: MOCCA is a randomized, phase 2 trial conducted in Singapore, Korea, and Australia. Eligible patients had rOCCC with recurrence after platinum-based chemotherapy, Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status ≤2, and no prior immune checkpoint blockade. Patients were randomly assigned (2:1) to durvalumab (1,500 mg every 4 weeks) or chemotherapy. Patients progressing on chemotherapy were allowed to cross over to durvalumab. The primary outcome was progression-free survival. Secondary outcomes included overall survival, objective response rates, and safety. Results: Forty-eight eligible women were assigned to durvalumab ( N = 31) or chemotherapy ( N = 17). The median progression-free survival was 7.6 [95% confidence interval (CI), 7.0–16.0] and 14.0 (95% CI, 7.0–32.9) weeks with durvalumab and chemotherapy, respectively (HR = 1.6; 95% CI, 0.8–3.0; P = 0.92). The median overall survival was 37.9 (95% CI, 21.7–143.0) and 40.6 (95% CI, 25.0–not reached) weeks, respectively (HR = 1.5; 95% CI, 0.7–3.3; P = 0.85). The difference in objective response rates between the groups was not statistically significant (durvalumab 9.7% vs. physician’s choice chemotherapy 18.8%; difference −9.1%; 95% CI, −31.3% to 12.9%; P = 0.83). Fewer all-grade (35.5% vs. 68.8%) and high-grade (9.7% vs. 31.3%) treatment-related adverse events were observed for durvalumab. PD-L1 combined positive score (CPS)+ was observed in 28.9% (CPS ≥1%) and 10.5% (CPS ≥10%) of patients. PIK3CA mutations were associated with time to progression on durvalumab ≥12 weeks [relative risk (mutated vs. wild-type) 2.83; 95% CI, 1.16–14.17]. Conclusions: Durvalumab was well-tolerated but did not improve efficacy outcomes compared with chemotherapy in rOCCC. Translational Relevance. Recurrent ovarian clear cell carcinoma (rOCCC) is an aggressive and rare subtype of ovarian cancer with no approved targeted therapy. Whereas anti–PD-(L)1 inhibitors in the subsequent-line setting have shown modest yet meaningful efficacy for this lethal disease in small, noncomparative trials, our study has presented for the first time that durvalumab is not superior to physician’s choice chemotherapy in rOCCC. To the best of our knowledge, the MOCCA trial represents the first randomized, comparative trial comparing the efficacy and safety of an immune checkpoint blockade approach with the previous standard of care in rOCCC. Durvalumab did not improve efficacy outcomes, including progression-free survival, objective response rate, or overall survival, compared with chemotherapy. However, durvalumab had a good safety profile and was well-tolerated in this patient population. Finally, our study demonstrates the importance of randomized, comparative trials, even in the setting of rare cancers. Introduction Ovarian clear cell carcinoma (OCCC) is a distinct subtype of epithelial ovarian cancer (EOC) with unique epidemiology, clinicopathologic features, gene expression profile, and immune microenvironment ( 1 – 5 ). Advanced OCCC has poor prognosis with previously described chemotherapy resistance. Subsequent-line objective response rates (ORR) to chemotherapy for recurrent OCCC (rOCCC) have been reported to range from 0% to 11% in retrospective series ( 6 – 8 ). The treatment of women with advanced rOCCC remains an area of significant unmet clinical need. Clinical trials over the past decade have investigated multikinase inhibitors targeting the VEGF pathway or the mTOR inhibitor temsirolimus as potential treatment strategies, without notable success ( 7 , 9 – 12 ), indicating a high demand for novel therapies for this difficult-to-treat disease. Several molecular characteristics provide rationale for immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in rOCCC. Deficiency in mismatch repair (MMR) protein expression occurs in up to 10% of patients with OCCC ( 4 , 13 – 16 ), whereas ARID1A mutations are prevalent in ∼50% of OCCC ( 17 , 18 ). ARID1A facilitates chromatin interactions with MSH2 during DNA repair, and loss of ARID1A function has been associated with a functional MMR-deficient phenotype ( 19 ). Further studies have described the expression of PD-L1 and PD-L2, as well as the upregulation of the IL-6–STAT3–HIF pathway in the tumor microenvironment of OCCC ( 20 , 21 ). IL-6 has been well described as an immunosuppressive cytokine in multiple tumor types by promoting PD-L1 expression ( 22 – 24 ), and circulating IL-6 was associated with response to nivolumab in patients with advanced melanoma ( 24 ). Furthermore, CD8 + tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes from OCCC samples showed reduced TOX expression, indicating less exhaustion, compared with those from high-grade serous ovarian cancer ( 25 ). Supporting this, preliminary data from the KEYNOTE-100 trial also suggested a signal of efficacy for ICB in rOCCC in which the response rate in patients with rOCCC to pembrolizumab was 15.8%, compared with only 8% in the overall EOC population ( 26 ). Similarly, on exploratory subgroup analysis of the NRG GY003 trial, patients with OCCC retrieved the most benefit from nivolumab plus ipilimumab and were associated with a fivefold odds of tumor response compared with non–clear cell EOC ( 27 ). We hypothesized that PD-L1 ICB may improve outcomes in rOCCC. MOCCA/APGOT-OV2/GCGS-OV3 investigated the efficacy of durvalumab, compared with physician’s choice chemotherapy (PCC), in patients with rOCCC. Patients and Methods Study design and participants This was a phase II, multicenter, randomized, open-label study to investigate the efficacy and safety of durvalumab as compared with PCC in patients with rOCCC, who were recruited from Singapore, South Korea, and Australia ( ClinicalTrials.gov ; NCT03405454 ). The study was reviewed and approved by local ethics review boards in each participating country and complied with the Declaration of Helsinki and Good Clinical Practice Guidelines. Written informed consent was obtained from each patient. The trial was sponsored by the National University Cancer Institute, Singapore. The study was reported in accordance with CONSORT guidelines. Eligible patients were ≥18 years old with rOCCC that had progressed or recurred after prior platinum-based chemotherapy. Patients were required to have histologically documented diagnosis of OCCC that was negative for WT1 expression by IHC. For tumors of mixed histology, ≥70% of the tumor must have consisted of OCCC. Other key inclusion criteria were as follows: ≤4 prior lines of systemic therapy; no prior treatment with ICB; an Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status (ECOG PS) of ≤2; presence of measurable disease; and provision of sufficient archival tumor for correlative analysis. The main exclusion criteria were as follows: previous grade ≥3 immune-related adverse event (irAE), active or prior autoimmune disease within the past 2 years, or multiple primary cancers. Procedures Enrolled patients were randomized in a 2:1 ratio to receive durvalumab or PCC after being stratified for ECOG PS (0, 1, or 2). The Cenduit Interactive Web Response System was used to generate the dynamic block randomization allocation sequence with a block size of six. Patients randomized to durvalumab received intravenous durvalumab dosed at 1,500 mg, once every 4 weeks. Patients randomized to PCC were allowed to receive any systemic cytotoxic chemotherapy chosen by the investigator; however, the addition of targeted therapy, including bevacizumab, or oral tyrosine kinase inhibitors was prohibited. Treatment in both groups continued until disease progression, intolerable toxicity, or withdrawal of patient consent for up to 24 months. Upon discontinuation of PCC due to disease progression or intolerable toxicity, patients randomized to PCC were allowed to cross over to receive durvalumab after completing a washout period of 4 weeks. At the end of study treatment, patients entered the 90-day follow-up phase. Outcomes The primary outcome was progression-free survival (PFS; Supplementary Data S1), which was evaluated using RECIST v1.1 for patients randomized to PCC and by RECIST v1.1 and modified RECIST for patients randomized to durvalumab. Secondary outcomes included the following: overall survival (OS; Supplementary Data S1); ORR; safety assessed by the type, grade, and frequency of treatment-related adverse events (TRAE); and irAEs. TRAEs were evaluated using the NCI Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events v4.0. An additional secondary outcome was quality of life (QoL), evaluated using the European Organization for Research and Treatment of Cancer Core QLQ-C30 and OV28 questionnaires, which were administered at baseline and at each treatment cycle. Planned imaging assessments for efficacy outcomes (CT or MRI) were performed every 8 weeks during treatment. The exploratory endpoint of this study was to identify predictive biomarkers of response and resistance to durvalumab. Statistical analysis The sample size was calculated based on the primary outcome measure of PFS. A retrospective analysis of median PFS in patients with rOCCC was 11 weeks ( 8 ). Hence, a HR of 0.50 was postulated assuming a median PFS of 10 weeks in the PCC arm and 20 weeks in the durvalumab arm. Based on a 6-month PFS probability of 15% in the PCC arm ( 8 ), a 2:1 treatment allocation, a one-sided 10% significance level, and a power of 80%, a sample size of 31 patients in the durvalumab arm and 16 patients in the PCC arm was required, giving a total sample size of 47 patients. This assumed the PFS followed an exponential distribution with an accrual of 2 years and a further follow-up of 2 years. The 6-month PFS probability, median PFS and OS duration, and their 95% confidence intervals (CI) were estimated using the Kaplan–Meier method with comparison of survival distributions made via the log-rank test. Further analysis based on Cox proportional hazards regression was conducted for the evaluation of PFS, with ECOG PS as the stratification factor. ORR measures were compared using the conditional exact binomial test and quantified in terms of difference in proportion with the 95% CIs estimated assuming the exact binomial distribution. QoL analyses were performed on patients who had QoL scores available at baseline and at least one visit from cycles two to seven. The mean difference in domain scores were assessed using a linear mixed model which was generated by including a random intercept and adjusting for the respective baseline QoL measure and time. Statistical analyses for the primary and secondary efficacy outcomes were conducted assuming a one-sided test at the 10% level of significance. All statistical analyses were performed using R (v4.2.1, r-project.org ). Translational analysis Translational endpoints of this trial were to identify predictive molecular markers of response to durvalumab. Genomic profiling was performed on archival formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded tumor samples by FoundationOneCDX (Foundation Medicine, Inc.). IHC for PD-L1 combined positive score (CPS %) was performed on suitable submitted archival formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded tumor samples. Patients who had insufficient archival tumor sample for sequencing had ARID1A (BAF250a) and MMR assessment performed by IHC. IHC was performed using a Ventana BenchMark XT system (RRID: SCR_026335) with antibodies against PD-L1 (Agilent, cat. # GE00621-2, RRID: AB_2833074), BAF250a (dilution 1:50, Sigma-Aldrich, cat. # HPA005456, RRID: AB_1078205), and MMR [MLH1 (Roche, cat. # 760-5091, RRID: AB_3669002), MSH2 (Roche, cat. # 790-5093, RRID: AB_2936886), MSH6 (Roche, cat. # 790-5092, RRID: AB_2936885), and PMS2 (Roche, cat# 790-5094, RRID: AB_3669003)]. Loss of BAF250a was defined as a lack of nuclear staining in 100% of tumor cells. CPS % was defined as the total number of tumors and immune cells stained with PD-L1 divided by the number of all viable tumor cells and then multiplied by 100. Categorical associations of each molecular marker with outcome to durvalumab, reported as relative risk (RR) and their 95% CIs, were performed using the conditional exact binomial test. In cases in which one of the comparison groups did not experience any event, the ‘adding one to each cell’ approach was utilized ( 28 ). Associations of molecular markers with PFS in durvalumab were performed using the log-rank test. Statistical analyses for the translational outcomes were exploratory and were conducted assuming a two-sided test at the 5% level of significance. Data availability The data generated in this study are not publicly available to avoid compromising patient privacy or consent but are available upon reasonable request from the corresponding author. Results Patient disposition Between November 7, 2017, and February 17, 2020, 57 patients were assessed for eligibility, and 48 were randomly assigned to treatment with durvalumab ( N = 31) or PCC ( N = 17). One patient who was randomized to PCC withdrew from the trial without receiving any treatment. Chemotherapy regimens received by patients randomized to PCC are described in Supplementary Table S1. A total of 47 patients were included in the intention-to-treat analyses ( Fig. 1 ). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Patient and treatment disposition. D, durvalumab. Baseline characteristics The median age (56 years), proportion of patients with ECOG PS 1 (45.2% vs. 37.5%), and proportion of patients receiving one prior treatment line (71.0% vs. 75.0%) were similar between patients randomized to receive durvalumab as compared with PCC ( Table 1 ). More ethnically Korean and Caucasian patients were randomized to receive durvalumab [(41.9% vs. 31.2%) and (19.4% vs. 6.2%), respectively]. Additionally, more patients in the durvalumab group had received prior bevacizumab (38.7% vs. 25.0%). Table 1. Baseline patient demographics and clinical characteristics. ​ PCC ( N = 16) Durvalumab ( N = 31) Overall ( N = 47) Age (years) ​ ​ ​ Median (range) 56 (36–81) 56 (35–70) 56 (35–81) Ethnicity ​ ​ ​ Caucasian 1 (6.2%) 6 (19.4%) 7 (14.9%) Chinese 8 (50.0%) 9 (29.0%) 17 (36.2%) Indian 1 (6.2%) 0 (0%) 1 (2.1%) Korean 5 (31.2%) 13 (41.9%) 18 (38.3%) Malay 0 (0%) 1 (3.2%) 1 (2.1%) Other 1 (6.2%) 2 (6.5%) 3 (6.4%) Country of trial ​ ​ ​ Australia 2 (12.5%) 7 (22.6%) 9 (19.1%) Korea 5 (31.2%) 12 (38.7%) 17 (36.2%) Singapore 9 (56.2%) 12 (38.7%) 21 (44.7%) ECOG PS ​ ​ ​ 0 10 (62.5%) 17 (54.8%) 27 (57.4%) 1 6 (37.5%) 14 (45.2%) 20 (42.6%) Cell type ​ ​ ​ Pure clear cell carcinoma 12 (92.3%) 23 (95.8%) 35 (94.6%) Mixed clear cell carcinoma 1 (7.7%) 1 (4.2%) 2 (5.4%) Number of prior treatment lines ​ ​ ​ 1 12 (75.0%) 22 (71.0%) 34 (72.3%) 2 2 (12.5%) 7 (22.6%) 9 (19.1%) 3 2 (12.5%) 1 (3.2%) 3 (6.4%) 4 0 (0%) 1 (3.2%) 1 (2.1%) Prior use of bevacizumab ​ ​ ​ Yes 4 (25.0%) 12 (38.7%) 16 (34.0%) No 12 (75.0%) 19 (61.3%) 31 (66.0%) Prior radiotherapy ​ ​ ​ Yes 0 (0.0%) 6 (19.4%) 6 (14.6%) No 16 (100.0%) 25 (80.6%) 41 (85.4%) Prior immune therapy ​ ​ ​ Yes ​ ​ ​ NK cell therapy 0 (0.0%) 1 (3.2%) 1 (2.1%) No 16 (100.0%) 30 (98.6%) 30 (97.9%) FIGO stage at diagnosis ​ ​ ​ 1 4 (25%) 9 (29%) 13 (27.7%) 2 3 (18.8%) 10 (32.3%) 13 (27.7%) 3 6 (37.5%) 6 (19.4%) 12 (25.5%) 4 3 (18.8%) 6 (19.4%) 9 (19.1%) Open in a new tab Information on cell type was missing for 3 (18.8%) patients in the PCC group and 7 (17.1%) in the durvalumab group. Abbreviation: FIGO, International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics. Efficacy outcomes Primary outcome At the data cutoff date of August 23, 2022, the median duration of follow-up was 126.7 weeks (IQR = 102–153.4) for durvalumab and 97 (IQR = 83–157.9) for PCC. All patients were included in the analysis for the duration that they were observed ( Fig. 1 ); this included one patient on the durvalumab arm who was lost to follow-up and censored ( Fig. 2A ; Supplementary Fig. S1). The median PFS was 7.6 weeks (95% CI, 7.0–16.0) in the durvalumab group compared with 14.0 weeks (95% CI, 7.0–32.9) in the PCC group (HR = 1.6; 95% CI, 0.8–3.0; one-sided log-rank P = 0.92; Fig. 2A and B ; Supplementary Fig. S1). A stratified analysis based on baseline ECOG PS did not alter the results (HR = 1.8; 95% CI, 0.9–3.7). The 6-month PFS rate was 10% (95% CI, 3.0%–28.0%) in the durvalumab group and 38% (95% CI, 20.0%–71.0%) in the PCC group. On post hoc subgroup analysis, median PFS duration was the longest amongst patients receiving platinum-based combination chemotherapy (Supplementary Fig. S2). It was 17.3 weeks [95% CI 7.0–not reached (NR)] for patients receiving platinum-based chemotherapy vs 10.7 weeks (95% CI, 7.0–NR) for patients receiving single-agent chemotherapy. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Antitumor activity of durvalumab (D) versus PCC. A, PFS assessed by RECIST v1.1 for D (blue) and PCC (red). PFS curves are curtailed at the 12-month mark. B, Univariate forest plot depicting post hoc subgroup analysis of PFS based on patient baseline characteristics. C, OS for D (blue) and PCC (red). OS curves are curtailed at the 36-month mark. D, Best change from baseline in target lesion size assessed by RECIST v1.1 in patients with ≥1 evaluable after baseline imaging assessment. Bars annotated with a star indicate patients who had the best overall response of progressive disease due to disease progression in nontarget lesions despite shrinkage or stability of target lesions. Bars annotated with a diamond indicate patients who had the best overall response of progressive disease due to unequivocal appearance of new lesions despite shrinkage or stability of target lesions. FIGO, International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics. Secondary efficacy outcomes The difference in OS between the durvalumab and PCC groups was not statistically significant ( Fig. 2C ). The median OS was 37.9 weeks (95% CI, 21.7–143.0) in the durvalumab group compared with 40.6 weeks (95% CI, 25.0 to NR) in the PCC group (HR = 1.5; 95% CI, 0.7–3.3; one-sided log-rank P = 0.85). On post hoc analysis, when patients who subsequently crossed over to receive durvalumab were excluded, no statistically significant difference in OS was observed between the durvalumab and PCC groups (HR = 1.34; 95% CI, 0.46–3.91; Supplementary Fig. S3). Furthermore, no significant difference in OS was observed between patients assigned to PCC without or with subsequent crossover to durvalumab (HR = 0.82; 95% CI, 0.22–3.08; Supplementary Fig. S3). There was no evidence of difference in ORRs between groups (durvalumab 9.7% vs. PCC 18.8%; −9.1% difference, 95% CI, −31.3% to 12.9%; P = 0.83; Fig. 2D ; Table 2 ). When patients who crossed over to receive durvalumab were included, the overall ORR to durvalumab was 12.5% (95% CI, 4.2%–26.8%) by RECIST v1.1 and 15.0% (95% CI, 5.7%–29.8%) by modified RECIST ( Table 2 ). Table 2. Clinical outcomes. RECIST v1.1 ​ PCC ( n = 16) Durvalumab (primary) ( n = 31) Durvalumab (crossover) ( n = 9) Durvalumab (all) ( n = 40) Difference [durvalumab (primary) – PCC] Best response (%) ​ ​ ​ ​ ​ CR 1 (6.2%) 0 (0%) 0 (0%) 0 (0%) — PR 2 (12.5%) 3 (9.7%) 2 (22.2%) 5 (12.5%) — SD 6 (37.5%) 9 (29.0%) 2 (22.2%) 11 (27.5%) — PD 7 (43.8%) 16 (51.6%) 4 (44.4%) 20 (50.0%) — NE 0 (0%) 3 (9.7%) 1 (11.1%) 4 (10.0%) — ORR (CR + PR) ​ ​ ​ ​ ​ 95% CI 3 (18.8%) 3 (9.7%) 2 (22.2%) 5 (12.5%) −9.1% ( P = 0.83) a ​ (4.0%–45.6%) (2.0%–25.8%) (2.8%–60.0%) (4.2%–26.8%) (−31.3% to 12.9%) Modified RECIST ​ Durvalumab (primary) ( n = 31) Durvalumab (crossover) ( n = 9) Durvalumab (all) ( n = 40) Best response (%) ​ ​ ​ CR 0 (0%) 0 (0%) 0 (0%) PR 3 (9.7%) 3 (33.3%) 6 (15%) SD 12 (38.7%) 4 (44.4%) 16 (40%) PD 10 (32.3%) 1 (11.1%) 11 (27.5%) NE 6 (19.4%) 1 (11.1%) 7 (17.5%) ORR (CR + PR) ​ ​ ​ 95% CI 3 (9.7%) 3 (33.3%) 6 (15%) ​ (2.0%–25.8%) (7.4%–70.1%) (5.7%–29.8%) Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CR, complete response; NE, not evaluable; PD, progressive disease; PR, partial response; SD, stable disease. a The P value was obtained from a one-sided conditional binomial exact test under the null hypothesis that the response rate in patients receiving durvalumab is lower than or equal to the response rate in patients receiving PCC and the alternative hypothesis that the response rate in patients receiving durvalumab is higher than the response rate in patients receiving PCC. Safety Durvalumab was well-tolerated with a lower incidence of all-grade (35.5% vs. 68.8%; RR, 0.52; 95% CI, 0.29–0.92) TRAEs compared with PCC. No TRAE leading to treatment discontinuation was observed on either arm. The most common TRAEs (all grades) experienced by patients randomized to durvalumab were fatigue (19.4%), hyperthyroidism (6.5%), rash (6.5%), and hyponatremia (6.5%; Table 3 ). Fewer grade 3 to 4 TRAEs occurred in patients receiving durvalumab compared with PCC (9.7% vs. 31.3%). Grade 3 to 4 TRAEs experienced by patients in the durvalumab group included one patient each who had rash (3.2%), increased serum amylase (3.2%), hyponatremia (3.2%), and hypomagnesemia (3.2%). In the PCC group, the most common grade 3 to 4 TRAEs were neutropenia (25.0%), anemia (12.5%), thrombocytopenia (6.2%), and oral mucositis (6.2%). irAEs of interest were observed in 14 of 31 patients treated with durvalumab (45.2%). No treatment-related deaths were observed in either group. Table 3. Frequency of TRAEs. Adverse event PCC ( n = 16) a Durvalumab ( n = 31) a,b Grade 3/4 All grades Grade 3/4 All grades Blood and lymphatic system disorders ​ ​ ​ ​ Anemia 2 (12.5%) 3 (18.8%) 0 0 Neutropenia 4 (25.0%) 6 (37.5%) 0 1 (3.2%) Thrombocytopenia 1 (6.2%) 3 (18.8%) 0 1 (3.2%) Endocrine disorders ​ ​ ​ ​ Hyperthyroidism 0 0 0 2 (6.5%) Hypothyroidism 0 0 0 1 (3.2%) Gastrointestinal disorders ​ ​ ​ ​ Abdominal pain 0 2 (12.5%) 0 1 (3.2%) Constipation 0 2 (12.5%) 0 0 Diarrhea 0 0 0 1 (3.2%) Gastritis 0 1 (6.2%) 0 0 Oral mucositis 1 (6.2%) 2 (12.5%) 0 0 Nausea 0 3 (18.8%) 0 1 (3.2%) Vomiting 0 1 (6.2%) 0 1 (3.2%) General disorders and administration site conditions ​ ​ ​ ​ Edema limbs 0 1 (6.2%) 0 1 (3.2%) Fatigue 0 6 (37.5%) 0 6 (19.4%) Fever 0 1 (6.2%) 0 0 Immune system disorders ​ ​ ​ ​ Allergic reaction 0 1 (6.2%) 0 0 Metabolism and nutrition disorders ​ ​ ​ ​ Alanine aminotransferase increased 0 0 0 1 (3.2%) Anorexia 0 1 (6.2%) 0 1 (3.2%) Aspartate aminotransferase increased 0