8 to −8.3; P = 0.001; Supplementary Fig. S4B), reduced peripheral neuropathy (mean difference of −10.2; 95% CI, −20.3 to −0.1; P = 0.024; Supplementary Fig. S4C), and less hair loss (mean difference of −19.5; 95% CI, −35.7 to −3.4; P = 0.010; Supplementary Fig. S4D). Translational analysis A total of 74.2% (23/31) and 75.0% (12/16) of patients randomized to durvalumab and PCC had successful FoundationOneCDX profiling (Supplementary Fig. S5A). Pathogenic genomic mutations were frequently noted in ARID1A (57.1%, 20/35), PIK3CA (45.7%, 16/35), KRAS (20.0%, 7/35), the promoter region of TERT (17.0%, 6/35), PPP2R1A (11.4%, 4/35) and PTEN (11.4%, 4/35; Supplementary Fig. S5A). As an exploratory analysis, when analyzing outcomes of all patients who received durvalumab, including those who crossed over to receive durvalumab after progression on PCC, pathogenic tumor mutations in PIK3CA were associated with longer time to progression on durvalumab ≥12 weeks (Supplementary Data S1), compared with those with tumors which were PIK3CA wild-type [RR (mutated vs. -wild-type) 2.83, 95% CI, 1.16–14.17; Supplementary Fig. S5B; Supplementary Table S3]. Pathogenic alterations in the PI3K pathway ( PIK3C2B , PIK3C2G , PIK3CA , PIK3R1 , RPTOR , and PTEN ) were associated with trend toward objective response to durvalumab (RR = 4.67; 95% CI, 0.96–14.00; Supplementary Fig. S5C; Supplementary Table S3). ERBB2 amplification was observed in 11.4% (4/35) of sequenced tumors (Supplementary Fig. S5A). Amongst durvalumab-assigned patients with successful tumor profiling, ERBB2 amplification was associated with worse PFS (log-rank HR = 3.87; 95% CI, 2.25–89.94; Supplementary Fig. S6). Loss of ARID1A expression or presence of ARID1A pathogenic mutation was not associated with durvalumab response or improved PFS. Eleven of 38 tested tumors (28.9%) were PD-L1–positive (≥1%) (Supplementary Figs. S1 and S5A). Four (10.5%) PD-L1–positive tumors showed a high PD-L1 CPS ≥10% (Supplementary Figs. S1 and S5A). All of 35 tested tumors were proficient in MMR protein expression or had microsatellite stability. Discussion This phase 2 randomized trial investigated durvalumab versus PCC in patients with rOCCC. Durvalumab did not improve outcomes of PFS, OS, or ORR compared with PCC but had a favorable safety profile. Whereas the hypothesis based on the primary endpoint was not met, the MOCCA trial represents the first randomized, comparative clinical trial investigating an immunotherapeutic approach versus chemotherapy. Few prospective data exist on the activity of chemotherapy in rOCCC, and the chemotherapy comparator group in this trial provides important data which may serve as key benchmarks for future clinical trial design. Other phase 2 trials of anti–PD-(L)1 ICB in recurrent OCCC have been conducted, including pembrolizumab ( 29 ) and nivolumab ( 30 ), overall demonstrating modest yet meaningful efficacy. In the PEACOCC trial which recruited 48 patients with advanced gynecologic clear cell carcinomas (85.4% OCCC), pembrolizumab showed an ORR of 22.9%, with a median PFS duration of 12.2 weeks (95% CI, 5.9–23.9) and a 12-week PFS rate of 43.8% (90% CI, 31.5–56.6; ref. 29 ). In the BrUOG354 trial, which randomized patients with ovarian and other extra-renal clear cell carcinomas in noncomparative assignment to nivolumab monotherapy or nivolumab plus ipilimumab, 78.6% of the 14 patients assigned to nivolumab monotherapy had OCCC ( 30 ). Amongst nivolumab monotherapy–treated patients, the ORR was 14.3%, median PFS duration was 2.2 months (95% CI, 1.2–3.4), and PFS at 6 months was 19.1% ( 30 ). Neither of these two trials included a comparator arm, and biomarker analyses from these trials are still awaited. Directly comparative data regarding the relative efficacy of anti–PD-L1 versus anti–PD-1 monotherapy approaches are lacking in rOCCC. However, a recent study suggested that PD-L2 may be the dominant ligand in the tumor microenvironment of advanced OCCC ( 5 ), showing that significantly more PD-L2 positivity was observed compared with PD-1 or PD-L1 by IHC in advanced OCCC samples and that PD-L2 expression was predominantly localized to the tumor cells within the malignant cell area ( 5 ). This may provide at least some rationale to support a theoretical advantage of PD-1–targeting immunotherapeutic approaches compared with those targeting PD-L1 in OCCC. Whereas PD-L2 expression was not evaluated on patient samples from the MOCCA trial, only 28.9% (11/38) and 10.5% (4/38) of successfully scored patients had a positive PD-L1 CPS exceeding the 1% and 10% thresholds, respectively, indicating that PD-L1 positivity by CPS was infrequent amongst patients enrolled in the present trial. Beyond anti–PD-(L)1 ICB as monotherapy, combinatorial immunotherapeutic strategies are attractive and may have a higher chance of success in rOCCC. Three phase 2 trials have now reported on the efficacy of the nivolumab plus ipilimumab combination in rOCCC, with the ORR ranging from 21.1% to 50.0% ( 30 – 32 ). Of note, the ipilimumab dosing schedule utilized on these trials differed, and the optimal dosing schedule for nivolumab plus ipilimumab in rOCCC should be further clarified. Two further phase 2 trials have also reported on the efficacy of combination strategies featuring an anti–PD-1 antibody plus an angiogenesis inhibitor in rOCCC, with ORRs of 40.5% and 44.0% reported for the sintilimab plus bevacizumab ( 33 ) and pembrolizumab plus lenvatinib ( 34 ) combinations, respectively. The efficacy observed for the aforementioned combinatorial immunotherapeutic approaches are highly promising, and further randomized, comparative trials of these treatment strategies, such as DOVE (APGOT-OV7/ENGOT-ov80 study; ref. 35 ), are strongly encouraged in rOCCC. The safety profile of durvalumab was consistent with the known profile of this drug, with no new safety signals observed. Grade 3 to 4 TRAEs occurred in only 9.7% of patients receiving durvalumab, which was notably lower than in patients receiving PCC. Durvalumab also led to no detriment to overall QoL results compared with chemotherapy when observed longitudinally over the first 6 months of the study and was associated with improvements in emotional functioning, constipation, hair loss, and peripheral neuropathy. On exploratory translational analyses, the genomic profile of OCCC in this study seemed concordant with previously reported literature, with frequent pathogenic alterations observed in ARID1A , PIK3CA , KRAS , and the promoter region of TERT ( 18 ). Notably, there remains a paucity of clinical data regarding potential genomic biomarkers of response and resistance to ICB in OCCC. Whereas a previous in vivo screen of cancer-associated somatic mutations (utilizing MC38 colon carcinoma mouse models) reported PI3K signaling (via PIK3CA H1047R hotspot mutation) to be a mechanism of resistance to PD-1 blockade by promoting an inhibitory myeloid microenvironment following immunotherapy ( 36 ), in the MOCCA study, pathogenic mutations in PIK3CA were conversely associated with longer time to progression amongst tumors from durvalumab-treated patients. Additionally, we found that ERBB2 amplification was associated with a significantly worse PFS amongst durvalumab-treated patients. These findings are hypothesis-generating, and further analysis of the tumor microenvironment in OCCC is warranted to understand the mechanisms underlying these associations. The main strengths of the MOCCA trial are its randomized, comparative design against common chemotherapies and its collaborative conduct in a rare and difficult-to-treat tumor type. Furthermore, the MOCCA trial enrolled patients exclusively in the Asia–Pacific region and recruited a multiethnic population comprising predominantly Chinese, Korean, and Caucasian patients but also a small number of Indian and Malay patients (Supplementary Table S4). Limitations of the present study include its relatively small sample size and one-sided hypothesis for efficacy and safety endpoints, as well as a lack of power to detect treatment differences between subgroups. In conclusion, durvalumab was well-tolerated with fewer TRAEs compared with PCC. However, durvalumab did not improve efficacy outcomes compared with PCC in patients with rOCCC. Supplementary Material Supplementary Data S1 Endpoint definitions ccr-25-0201_supplementary_data_s1_suppfs1.docx (19.3KB, docx) Supplementary Figure S1 Supplementary Figure S1 ccr-25-0201_supplementary_figure_s1_suppfs1.png (994KB, png) Supplementary Figure S2 Supplementary Figure S2 ccr-25-0201_supplementary_figure_s2_suppfs2.png (471KB, png) Supplementary Figure S3 Supplementary Figure S3 ccr-25-0201_supplementary_figure_s3_suppfs3.png (229KB, png) Supplementary Figure S4 Supplementary Figure S4 ccr-25-0201_supplementary_figure_s4_suppfs4.png (579KB, png) Supplementary Figure S5 Supplementary Figure S5 ccr-25-0201_supplementary_figure_s5_suppfs5.png (1.1MB, png) Supplementary Figure S6 Supplementary Figure S6 ccr-25-0201_supplementary_figure_s6_suppfs6.png (93.4KB, png) Supplementary Table S1 Chemotherapy regimens received by patients randomized to physician’s choice chemotherapy ccr-25-0201_supplementary_table_s1_suppts1.docx (13KB, docx) Supplementary Table S2 Longitudinal analyses of EORTC QLQ-C30 and QLQ-OV28 scores, comparing physician’s choice chemotherapy (red) and durvalumab (blue); PCC: physician’s choice chemotherapy; D: durvalumab; QoL: quality of life; GI: gastrointestinal ccr-25-0201_supplementary_table_s2_suppts2.docx (16.2KB, docx) Supplementary Table S3 Pathogenic alterations in PIK3CA or genes encoding the PI3K pathway versus -wildtype in relation to durvalumab benefit ccr-25-0201_supplementary_table_s3_suppts3.docx (14.4KB, docx) Supplementary Table S4 Representativeness of Study Participants ccr-25-0201_supplementary_table_s4_suppts4.docx (13.7KB, docx) Acknowledgments We thank the patients and families for their participation in the MOCCA 