INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1730,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11464969','A case report of mucinous borderline ovarian tumor with recurrence as invasive carcinoma with high copy number alterations.','Wakazono E, Taki M, Watanabe K, Yamanoi K, Murakami R, Kakiuchi N, Yamaguchi K, Hamanishi J, Minamiguchi S, Ogawa S, Mandai M.','2024','mbots mbots mocs mbot mbot mbot mbot mbot mbot s13691 mbots mbots mbots mbot mbot mbot adnexectomy intraperitoneally mbot pt1c2nxmx ic2 pax8 cdx2 imp3 mbot pax8 cdx2 imp3 mbot mbot microdissection g12c mbot g12d e154k a21gfs mbot mbot 12p 17p mbot mbot mbots mbots mbots mbot mbot mbot g12c mocs mbot mbots mocs mbot mbot mbot mbot mbot mbot mbots mocs mbots mbot mbots mocs mbots mocs mbots mbots 13691_2024_722_moesm1_esm 13691_2024_722_moesm2_esm mbot pax8 cdx2 imp3 100μm jst jpmjfr215v moonshot jpmjms2022 amed evolutional jp22gm1110011 moonshot jp22zf0127009 morice gouy mucinoud kurnit frumovitx mink gorringe cheasley hauptmann redline zeissig ashwood kondrashova batter g12d gemignani schlaerth bogomolniy therachiyil azmi f1000res cheasley ryland e10877 ismam cheasley lepage kobel ronnett s123 s131 13691_2024_722_moesm1_esm 13691_2024_722_moesm2_esm mbot pax8 cdx2 imp3 100μm','Abstract Mucinous borderline ovarian tumors (MBOTs) have a very low recurrence rate and a good prognosis, especially in the early stages, but some MBOTs occasionally recur with the progression to mucinous ovarian carcinomas (MOCs). Here, we present a case of MBOT that recurred as invasive MOC within 3 years. To examine the reason for the progression from MBOT to MOC, whole-exome sequencing of our case identified identical mutations and copy number alterations in KRAS , CDKN2A , and TP53 in both the MBOT and recurrent MOC. The recurrent MOC had a greater copy number alteration burden compared to the primary MBOT. These findings suggest that MBOT may have progressed to MOC via recurrence, wherein the increased burden of copy number alterations could be its key driver. It was also suggested that TP53 mutations already present in MBOT may contribute to the increased copy number alterations leading to MOC. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1007/s13691-024-00722-1. Keywords: Mucinous borderline ovarian tumor, Mucinous ovarian carcinoma, TP53 , Copy number alterations Introduction Mucinous ovarian tumors show clear progression from benign, to borderline, and then to carcinoma [ 1 ]. Mucinous ovarian carcinoma (MOC) is a rare subtype of epithelial ovarian carcinoma [ 2 ]. It is often detected at an early stage and has a good prognosis; however, its prognosis at advanced stage or recurrence with peritoneal dissemination was severe because MOC is refractory to conventional platinum-based chemotherapy regimens [ 3 , 4 ]. Mucinous borderline ovarian tumors (MBOTs) rarely recur, usually after a prolonged period of more than 5 years, with the prognosis typically being good [ 3 ]. Furthermore, even after recurrence, they often remain as MBOTs and do not progress to MOC, though some of MBOTs sometimes recur with the progression to MOC. Here, we report a case of MBOT that recurred as invasive MOC with multiple peritoneal metastases, approximately 2 years after the initial surgery. We also investigated gene mutations and copy number alterations in three components (i.e., cystadenoma and MBOT at initial onset, and MOC at recurrence) to gain insight into its underlying biology and the reasons for its switch from MBOT to MOC. Case report Clinical course A 73-year-old woman, who was on peritoneal dialysis for chronic renal failure due to immunoglobulin A nephropathy and was under observation for 10 years at our hospital for a right ovarian tumor of 11 cm in size, suddenly developed right lower abdominal pain (Supplementary Fig. 1A). On the same day, her peritoneal dialysis drainage volume increased; a laparoscopic bilateral adnexectomy was performed with suspected rupture of the right ovarian tumor. Intraperitoneally, the right ovarian tumor had ruptured and her left ovary was normal. The final pathological diagnosis was a MBOT of the right ovary, pT1c2NXMX, stage IC2, with a mucinous cystadenoma–borderline tumor sequence (Fig. 1 A, B ). Immunohistochemical analysis of both components of the ovarian tumor (cystadenoma and borderline tumor components) showed positive staining for PAX8 and negative staining for CDX2, indicating that the ovarian tumor was derived from the ovaries (Supplementary Fig. 2A and B and Table 1 ). p16 (CDKN2A) was only expressed in the cystadenoma (Fig. 1 C). The borderline component showed wild-type pattern, but focally diffuse with the basal layer of the borderline tumor cells, for p53 nuclear staining (Fig. 1 D). An insulin-like growth factor mRNA-binding protein 3 (IMP3), a marker for an ovarian mucinous borderline tumor and carcinoma, was expressed only in borderline component (Supplementary Fig. 2C). Since the patient was on peritoneal dialysis, we decided to follow-up the patient without additional surgery or treatment. Two years later, the patient was switched from peritoneal dialysis to hemodialysis. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab The pathological findings of the primary tumor. A The initial mucinous borderline tumor (MBOT) with mucinous cystadenoma by hematoxylin–eosin (HE) staining. B Left; cystadenoma, right; borderline tumor by HE staining. C p16 (CDKN2A) cytoplasmic immunostaining. D p53 nuclear immunostaining. Scale bars: A , C , D 100 μm, B 50 μm Table 1. The immunohistochemical patterns of three components PAX8 CDX2 IMP3 p16 (CDKN2A) p53 Ki67 A. Cystadenoma + , 80% – – + , diffuse Wild/ < 5% 0% B. Borderline + , 30% + , 20% + , 100% Null Wild/, focal, diffuse < 10%, hot spot: 25% (181/354) C. Carcinoma + , 30% – + , 100% Null Wild/50% Hot spot: 46% (100/219) Open in a new tab (A) Component of mucinous cystadenoma of the primary tumor. (B) Component of mucinous borderline ovarian tumor (MBOT) of the primary tumor. (C) Mucinous ovarian carcinoma (MOC) of the recurrent tumor Thirty-one months after initial onset, the patient was urgently admitted to the hospital because of increasing ascites effusion. Following ascites puncture, cancer cells were detected. Position emission tomography (PET)–CT suggested carcinomatous peritonitis (supplementary Fig. 1B). Exploratory laparoscopy revealed that the entire abdominal wall and ascending colon was covered with white peritoneal metastasis and a subcutaneous nodule was observed in the left lower abdomen (Supplementary Fig. 1B). The peritoneal and subcutaneous nodules were sampled, wherein MOC was detected in both lesions (Fig. 2 A and B ). The immunostaining pattern was similar to that in the borderline component at 1st surgery, wherein Ki67 labeling index within the hot spots was increased from 25 to 46% and 50% of tumor cells were positive for p53 nuclear staining, still remained wild-type pattern (Fig. 2 C, D and Supplementary Fig. 2). The patient was diagnosed with the recurrence of MBOT with the progression to MOC. The patient died 32 months after the initial onset and 1 month after recurrence. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab The pathological findings of the recurrent mucinous carcinoma (MOC). A , C and D the peritoneal metastasis and ( B ) the subcutaneous tumor. A and B The recurrent mucinous carcinoma (MOC) by HE staining. C p16 (CDKN2A) cytoplasmic immunostaining. ( D ) p53 nuclear immunostaining. Scale bars: A , C and D 100 μm B 50 μm Whole-exome sequencing DNA was extracted from paraffin-embedded sections using a laser-capture microdissection system. Whole-exome sequencing was performed for three specimens: cystadenoma and borderline components at 1st surgery as well as carcinoma at recurrence. Ovarian stroma was used as a germline control. All the tumors shared many genetic mutations, indicating that they originated from the same clone (Fig. 3 A, B ). Cystadenoma displayed only the p.G12C somatic mutation in the KRAS gene as a driver mutation, whereas both MBOT at initial onset and MOC at recurrence shared driver events, including the p.G12D mutation in KRAS , p.E154K mutation in TP53, and p.A21Gfs*24 mutation in CDKN2A . The patient had no pathological germline mutations. The proportion of the genome displaying copy number alterations increased stepwise from mucinous cystadenoma to MBOT to MOC (Fig. 3 C). MBOT and MOC shared a gain of 12p including KRAS and losses of heterozygosity of CDKN2A (9p) and TP53 (17p), despite wild-type pattern of p53 nuclear staining. We found whole-genome doubling and more copy number alterations including amplification of the KRAS mutant allele only in MOC, which suggests one of the reasons for the progression from MBOT to MOC. Fig. 3. Open in a new tab The genetic analyses of three components in our case. A Somatic mutations and their variant allele frequencies in three components; mucinous cystadenoma, borderline tumor (MBOT) in the initial onset tumor and recurrent mucinous carcinoma (MOC). Each column represents an equivalent mutation. Driver mutations are illustrated. B Clonal ordering based on somatic mutations and copy number aberrations. Driver mutations in each clone were noted. C Copy number aberration analysis based on whole-exome sequencing data. Blue dots represent total copy numbers. Green and red dots represent the B-allelic frequencies. Black lines on blue dots represent copy number changes Discussion MBOTs may recur but are rarely fatal. Most MBOTs are stage I, with a survival rate of almost 100% with or without intraepithelial carcinoma [ 5 ]. For MOC progress from benign epithelium to a pre-invasive lesion to carcinoma with shared genetic events, MBOTs often have components of benign cystadenoma, MBOT, and MOC. Considering the possibility of inadequate sampling in our case, MBOT at the 1st surgery were re-sectioned and retrospectively examined, and a continuum of MBOT was found from mucinous cystadenoma, but no malignant component was found. To investigate why it recurred with the progression to MOC, we examined genetic mutations and copy number alterations in the cystadenoma, borderline tumor, and carcinoma components. All three shared somatic mutations (Fig. 2 ), suggesting that they were derived from the same clone. Mucinous cystadenoma showed only a p.G12C mutation in KRAS as a driver mutation, whereas borderline tumors and recurrent MOCs shared driver mutations in KRAS , TP53, and CDKN2A . Some gene mutation data were consistent with the immunostaining patterns of the components (Fig. 1 ). However, p53 nuclear staining showed wild-type pattern, albeit focally diffuse, in both MBOT and recurrent MOC. KRAS and CDKN2A mutations were found in 95% of MBOTs and 80–90% of MOCs, which were not thought to be the key drivers of progression to MOC [ 6 – 9 ]. In contrast, as key drivers of progression from borderline precursors to invasive MOC, TP53 mutations and copy number alterations have been previously reported by genetic analyses of MOC, including cystadenoma and borderline tumors [ 9 ]. TP53 mutation has been reported as the key driver in a previously reported case of a ruptured MBOT with rapid recurrence as an invasive MOC of the contralateral ovary [ 10 ]. In addition, a high copy number alteration burden was associated with a worse prognosis in MOC [ 9 ]. In our case, the TP53 mutation already existed in MBOT; the only difference was that the recurrent MOC had more copy number alterations and whole-genome doubling. Copy number alterations contribute to carcinogenesis and TP53 mutations have been suggested to be associated with copy number alterations [ 11 ]. Given that TP53 mutations increase copy number alterations, borderline tumors with TP53 mutations may be considered cancer-like. Kang et al. reported that TP53 mutation in MBOT was associated with a higher risk of recurrence and overall survival (HR = 4.6, 95% CI 1.5–14.3, p = 0.0087), whereas TP53 mutation in MOC was not associated with overall survival [ 12 ]. Our case suggest that increased copy number alterations could contribute to carcinogenesis from MBOT to MOC, and that TP53 mutation may support this. Immunohistochemical nuclear staining for p53 is usually performed to examine TP53 mutation status since TP53 target sequencing is not routinely clinically accessible. In our case, MBOT was diagnosed with wild-type pattern for p53 nuclear staining because p53 positive cells were focally diffuse with the basal layer of the MBOT, not positive in the superficial cells. Kang et al. reported that the criteria for endometrial carcinoma and high grade serous ovarian carcinoma were not concordant in MBOTs and MOCs to account for terminal differentiation and intratumoral heterogeneity [ 12 , 13 ]. They suggest that the cases with a minimum of overexpression in 5% of tumor cell nuclei within the basal layer of MBOTs be regarded as abnormal p53 immunohistochemistry pattern, because the cases with a TP53 missense mutation showed overexpression in 5 to 100% of tumor cell nuclei. According to their criteria, MBOT in our case should have been diagnosed with p53 mutation pattern since more than 5% of borderline tumor cells within the basal layer of borderline tumor cells were positive for p53 staining, compared to mucinous cystadenoma component (Fig. 1 D). In conclusion, MBOTs sometimes recur as invasive MOCs. High copy number alterations can contribute to the progression of MBOTs to MOCs, especially if MBOTs have TP53 mutations. For MBOTs with TP53 mutations, careful follow-up may be advisable, given the possibility of recurrence as invasive MOC refractory to chemotherapy. Supplementary Information Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. 13691_2024_722_MOESM1_ESM.tif (4.2MB, tif) Supplementary file1 Supplementary fig. 1. Images of the initial tumor and the recurrent tumor. (A) The initial tumor, top: T2-weighted magnetic resonance image (MRI), bottom: macroscopic images, (B) The recurrent mucinous carcinoma, top: PET-CT images, bottom left: T2-weighted MRI and bottom right: intraperitoneal image. (TIF 4293 KB) 13691_2024_722_MOESM2_ESM.tif (6.9MB, tif) Supplementary file2 Supplementary fig. 2. The immunostaining findings of the initial tumor and the recurrent tumor. (A-D) The initial mucinous borderline tumor (MBOT) with mucinous cystadenoma. (E-H) The recurrent mucinous carcinoma (MOC). (A, E) PAX8 nuclear staining, (B, F) CDX2 nuclear staining, (C, G) IMP3 cytoplasmic immunostaining, and (D, H) Ki67 nuclear immunostaining. Scale bars: 100μm. (TIF 7049 KB) Funding This research was funded by the Japan Science and Technology Agency (JST): Fusion Oriented REsearch for disruptive Science and Technology (FOREST) Program (JPMJFR215V to N.K.), the Moonshot Research and Development Program (JPMJMS2022 to N.K.), the Japan Agency for Medical Research and Development (AMED): The Core Research for Evolutional Science and Technology (CREST) (grant no. JP22gm1110011 to S.O.) and the Moonshot Research and Development Program (grant no. JP22zf0127009 to S.O.). Data availability The datasets used during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Conflict of interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest related to this manuscript. Ethical approval All procedures performed in studies involving human participants were in accordance with the ethical standards of the institutional and/or national research committee and with the 1964 Helsinki declaration and its later amendments or comparable ethical standards. For this type of study, formal consent is not required. Informed consent Informed consent was obtained from the participant included in the study. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Morice P, Gouy S, Leary A (2019) Mucinoud ovarian carcinoma. N Engl J Med 380:2356–2266 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Kurnit KC, Frumovitx M (2022) Primary mucinous ovarian cancer: options for surgery and chemotherapy. Int J Gynecol Cancer 32:1455–1462 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Sherman ME, Mink PJ, Curtis R et al (2004) Survival among women with borderline ovarian tumors and ovarian carcinoma: a population-based analysis. Cancer 100:1045–1052 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Gorringe KL, Cheasley D, Wakefield MJ et al (2020) Therapeutic options for mucinous ovarian carcinoma. Gynecol Oncol 156:552–560 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Hauptmann S, Friedrich K, Redline R et al (2017) Ovarian borderline tumors in the 2014 WHO classification: evolving concepts and diagnostic criteria. Virchows Arch 470:125–142 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Zeissig MN, Ashwood LM, Kondrashova O et al (2023) Next batter up! Targeting cancers with KRAS-G12D mutations. Trends Cancer 9:955–967 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Gemignani ML, Schlaerth AC, Bogomolniy F et al (2003) Role of KRAS and BRAF gene mutations in mucinous ovarian carcinoma. Gynecol Oncol 90:378–381 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Therachiyil L, Anand A, Azmi A et al (2022) Role of RAS signaling in ovarian cancer. F1000Res 11:1253 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Cheasley D, Wakefield MJ, Ryland G et al (2019) The molecular origin and taxonomy of mucinous ovarian carcinoma. Nat Commun 10:3935 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Yang Y, Guan Y, Xu M et al (2022) Rapid recurrence of a ruptured mucinous borderline ovarian tumor harboring. Heliyon 8:e10877 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Steele CD, Abbasi A, Ismam SMA et al (2022) Signatures of copy number alterations in human cancer. Nature 606:984–991 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Kang EY, Cheasley D, LePage C et al (2021) Refined cut-off for TP53 immunohistochemistry improves prediction of TP53 mutation status in ovarian mucinous tumors: implications for outcome analyses. Mod Pathol 34:194–206 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Köbel M, Ronnett BM, Singh N et al (2019) Interpretation of P53 immunohistochemistry in endometrial carcinomas: toward increased reproducibility. Int J Gynecol Pathol 38(Suppl 1):S123–S131 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials 13691_2024_722_MOESM1_ESM.tif (4.2MB, tif) Supplementary file1 Supplementary fig. 1. Images of the initial tumor and the recurrent tumor. (A) The initial tumor, top: T2-weighted magnetic resonance image (MRI), bottom: macroscopic images, (B) The recurrent mucinous carcinoma, top: PET-CT images, bottom left: T2-weighted MRI and bottom right: intraperitoneal image. (TIF 4293 KB) 13691_2024_722_MOESM2_ESM.tif (6.9MB, tif) Supplementary file2 Supplementary fig. 2. The immunostaining findings of the initial tumor and the recurrent tumor. (A-D) The initial mucinous borderline tumor (MBOT) with mucinous cystadenoma. (E-H) The recurrent mucinous carcinoma (MOC). (A, E) PAX8 nuclear staining, (B, F) CDX2 nuclear staining, (C, G) IMP3 cytoplasmic immunostaining, and (D, H) Ki67 nuclear immunostaining. Scale bars: 100μm. (TIF 7049 KB) Data Availability Statement The datasets used during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.',100,'2025-10-13 06:59:38','2025-10-13 06:59:38');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1731,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11465014','A case of grade1 follicular lymphoma diagnosed by laparoscopic lymph node resection: differentiating from late lymph node recurrence of endometrial cancer.','Koyama S, Okamoto H, Yamanoi K, Mizuno R, Sunada M, Taki M, Murakami R, Ito H, Yamaguchi K, Hamanishi J, Mandai M.','2024','posiotive enlarging gravida obturator adnexectomy t1n1m0 pstageiia pamidronate obturator obturator obturator obturator resect obturator incised uneventful laparoscopically cd10 cd10 enlargements amold profuse sweats waxing hiraoka axln eto axln mirkheshi papanastasopoulos michalinos laudenschlager axln axln barranger takahara yoshino axln axially resect laparoscopies ureters pulsation peristalsis haruko koji yamanoi nakai higashi kakuwa s10147 yoshino kurita jog nab s430273 matsuura matoda yamanoi okunomiya s13691 brunette bonyadlou valkema noordman wijnhoven bpl assi alameh rockall barwick fec makis mesenteritis panniculitis valls badve blre nakajo nakajo faudemer textural s12935 weisenburger altshuler richhart e931772 ajcr leccisotti maccora malafronte watchful karam cyriac deoxyglucose s12149 jcm12041417 abdominopelvic s00428 takahara nagae yamagata bhanote choksi cassar yoshino shiomi s12957 hiraoka masumoto s40792 eto mco mirkheshti mohebtash papanastasopoulos tbj michalinos vassilakopoulos levidou coincidence laudenschlager geis lunt barranger marpeau uzan nonsentinel 122x','Abstract Follicular lymphoma is a common hematologic malignancy; however, it is less common among all malignant diseases and is difficult to suspect in advance due to the lack of specific clinical findings. Here, we report a case in which a late recurrence of corpus cancer was first suspected and finally diagnosed as follicular lymphoma. A 67-year-old female presented to our department with enlarged pelvic lymph nodes. She was diagnosed with breast cancer (HER2-posiotive with lymph node metastasis) and corpus cancer (endometrioid carcinoma grade 2, stage IA) 16 years prior, received definitive therapy and was followed up. A positron emission tomography scan was performed, and an accumulation of 18F-fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) was detected in multiple lymph nodes, including the lymph nodes with no change in size or enlargement. We performed laparoscopic resection of the enlarged and FDG-accumulated lymph nodes and a pathological examination. The patient was diagnosed with follicular lymphoma (FL) grade 1 and is currently under observation at the Department of Hematology. FL can be considered when there is a discrepancy between the change in lymph node size and the degree of FDG accumulation. A pathological examination is useful for accurate diagnosis. Therefore, it is important to consider tissue collection; however, care must be taken to minimize the invasiveness of the procedure for the patient. Keywords: Corpus cancer, Low-grade follicular lymphoma, FDG accumulation, Laparoscopy, Distinguishing from late recurrence Introduction Uterine corpus cancer is the most frequently occurring gynecological tumor, and its incidence has been increasing in recent years [ 1 ]. The most common histological type is endometrioid grade 1/2, which is categorized as type I and accounts for approximately 80% of all cases with a relatively good prognosis [ 2 ]. However, although rare, late recurrence in type-I endometrial carcinoma has been reported 5 years or more after the completion of treatment [ 3 , 4 ]. As previously reported, lymph nodes can occasionally be the site of late recurrence [ 5 ]; therefore, a method for systemic surveillance should be employed during follow-up. Imaging tests are very useful and widely used to detect tumor metastasis and recurrence. Positron emission tomography (PET), which detects 18F-fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) accumulation, is commonly used in routine clinical practice because of its high sensitivity and relative ease of interpretation [ 6 – 9 ]. However, imaging tests inevitably produce false positives at certain frequencies. The known causes of false positives include infectious, autoimmune, and hematological diseases [ 10 – 12 ]. Although there are various types of hematological diseases, low-grade hematological malignancies that cause localized lymph node enlargement are relatively uncommon, making them less likely to be considered in clinical practice. To the best of our knowledge, there have been few cases of difficulties in differentiating the late recurrence of corpus cancer from hematological diseases. In the present case, the patient had been treated for corpus cancer more than 10 prior and had slowly enlarging pelvic lymph nodes. A PET scan was performed for a detailed examination, which revealed significant FDG accumulation not only in the enlarged lymph nodes but also in other un-changed pelvic lymph nodes. The patient was finally diagnosed with low-grade follicular lymphoma (FL) based on a histopathological examination. Case presentation A 67-year-old woman, gravida 1, para 1, came to our department, after having been found to have enlarged right external iliac and obturator lymph nodes during follow-up after treatment for breast cancer and corpus cancer. The patient was diagnosed with breast cancer and uterine cancer simultaneously 16 years prior. Treatment and follow-up of corpus cancer and breast cancer In X year, she was referred to our department with a mass in her corpus. After a detailed examination, she was found to have corpus cancer (endometrioid cancer grade 1) and left breast cancer. Corpus cancer was clinically diagnosed as stage IA, and a simple hysterectomy and bilateral adnexectomy were performed in November X year. A pathological examination revealed an endometrioid carcinoma grade 2 with no lymph vascular invasion and thin myometrial invasion. No further treatment was recommended for the corpus cancer, and the patient was followed up. Treatment for the left breast cancer began in January the following year. A partial left mastectomy and left axillary dissection were performed. A pathological examination revealed negative results for estrogen and progesterone receptors, positive HER2 staining, and lymph node metastasis as pathological stage T1N1M0 pStageIIA. The patient was followed up without postoperative treatment; however, metastasis to the right eighth rib was detected 2 years after the initial surgery. Radiotherapy and chemotherapy (trastuzumab, pamidronate, and capecitabine) were administered and a complete response (CR) was confirmed after 9 months. Thereafter, the patient was followed up with trastuzumab for 5 years, and CR was maintained. Thereafter, the patient was followed up in the breast cancer unit without any medication. Treatment course after detecting enlarged pelvic lymph nodes After pelvic lymph node enlargement was noted in November X + 16 years, images taken on March X + 15 years and March X + 16 years were reviewed (Fig. 1 A, B, C, respectively). We observed that the size of the lymph nodes gradually increased. Considering the location of the tumor, a late recurrence of the corpus cancer was suspected, and positron emission tomography (PET) was performed to check for other metastases. A FDG accumulation was found in multiple lymph nodes. The SUVmax values for the lymph nodes in our case were as follows: left external iliac 4.8, left obturator 3.4, right obturator 2.9, and right external iliac 4.3. This includes not only the sites of the swollen lymph nodes but also other lesions with unchanged lymph nodes (Fig. 2 B, C shows increasingly enlarged lymph nodes; Fig. 2 D, E shows unchanged lymph nodes). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Changes in Computed tomography (CT) imaging findings. A CT image taken on November X + 16 years. B CT image taken on March X + 15 years. C CT image taken on March X + 16 years. Yellow circle indicates the gradually increasing lymph nodes Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Positron emission tomography (PET) findings. A Distribution of FDG accumulation in the abdominal cavity. B – E Fusion image of FDG accumulation and CT. B FDG accumulation with increasing size at the right external iliac lymph node site (Red arrow). SUVmax 4.3. C FDG accumulation with increasing size at the right obturator lymph node site (Red arrow). SUVmax 2.9. D FDG accumulation with un-changed size at the left obturator lymph node site (Blue arrow). SUVmax 3.4. E FDG accumulation with un-changed size at the left external iliac lymph node site (Blue arrow). SUVmax 4.8 FDG accumulation was predictive of some malignant diseases, but the heterogeneity of the lymph node size changes suggested diseases other than the late recurrence of corpus cancer. To correctly identify the pathophysiology, a pathological examination was recommended. We planned to resect the right external iliac and obturator lymph nodes, where both size changes and FDG accumulation were observed. In the CT images, we confirmed the precise location of the lymph nodes using the positions of the common, external, and internal iliac arteries and right ureter as landmarks (Fig. 3 ). Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Detailed examination of targeted lymph node site. Red dotted circle indicates the position of targeted lymph nodes. A Images of equilibrium phase CT. B Images of arterial phase CT. The target lymph node located on the cranial side was situated near the internal and external iliac arteries branches, lateral to the common iliac vein. C Images of arterial phase CT. D Images of equilibrium phase CT. The caudal target lymph node was located posterior to the external iliac vein and lateral to the internal iliac artery Subsequently, a laparoscopic surgery was performed. The dorsal peritoneum was incised and the common iliac artery was identified. The branches of the internal and external iliac arteries, right ureter, and right external iliac vein were identified (Fig. 4 A, B). The lymph node site to be resected was revealed, and the entire surrounding fatty tissue, including the lymph nodes, was dissected from the external iliac artery and vein (Fig. 4 C, D). The patient had an uneventful postoperative course and was discharged 5 days later. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Intraoperative findings. A , B Both the target lymph nodes were identified at the same location as inferred from the prior CT images. C , D Both target lymph nodes were correctly dissected laparoscopically The histopathological examination revealed a follicular pattern distributed throughout the lymph nodes, leading to effacement of the architecture (Fig. 5 A). Immunohistochemical staining showed Bcl-2 (+), CD10 (+), CD20 (+), and a Ki67 index of 30% (Fig. 5 B, C, D, respectively). Based on these findings, the patient was diagnosed with follicular lymphoma grade 1, Stage IIIA. The patient was referred to the hematology department, where it was determined that no therapeutic intervention was necessary and was followed up. Three and a half years have passed since the diagnosis, and the patient remains in stable disease, with no tumor enlargement or emergence of new lesions. Fig. 5. Open in a new tab Pathological findings. Black bar indicates 100 um. A Hematoxylin and Eosin staining, × 100 magnification. The neoplastic follicle extending beyond the nodal capsule. B – E Immunohistochemical results: positive for Bcl-2 ( B ), CD10 ( C ), CD20 ( D ) and Ki-67 labelling index 30% ( E ) Discussion In the present case, we encountered multiple pelvic lymph node enlargements in a patient who developed breast and corpus cancers 16 years before. Late recurrence of both breast and corpus cancers has been encountered frequently, and we initially assumed that the patient had a late recurrence of corpus cancer considering the location of the enlarged lymph nodes. However, a PET scan was performed, and an accumulation of FDG was observed even in areas with little change in size, making a diagnosis based on imaging findings alone extremely difficult. As described above, although PET scans are useful for detecting cancer metastases, false positives inevitably occur. False positives can be caused by hematologic diseases, including FL, a low-grade B-cell non-HL disease [ 13 , 14 ]. Amold F, et al. reported that more than 80% of FL patients do not have the systemic symptoms (so-called B symptoms: fever, weight loss, and profuse night sweats) seen in malignant lymphomas or laboratory abnormalities, such as decreased blood counts and elevated LDH [ 15 , 16 ]. The imaging findings of FL are characterized by annual waxing and waning and relatively FDG accumulation [ 17 ]. The degree of FDG accumulation is a clinically significant finding, as some studies suggest that it may indicate the malignancy of follicular lymphoma (FL) [ 18 – 20 ]. In this case, the SUV values were not unusually high for FL grade 1. Conversely, Hyun JK et al. investigated SUVmax values in lymph node metastases from endometrial carcinoma, reporting a range of 1.4–37.7, with an average of 9.3 [ 21 ]. The SUVmax values observed in our case were somewhat lower than these reported figures. Considering the minimal size changes and the SUVmax values, it is possible that follicular lymphoma could have been considered as a potential diagnosis in this case. Nevertheless, the incidence of FL in Japan is estimated to be approximately 3.28 in 100,000 [ 22 ]. Y, Huang, et al. reported that the incidence of incidental lymphoma detected during lymphadenectomy in the abdomen was only 0.06% [ 23 ]. In this study, we conducted a literature search for cases of malignant tumors coexisting with FL Grade 1. As shown in Table 1 , the number of reported cases was limited to ten, even for breast cancer, which is the most frequent malignancies [ 18 ]. Incidental cases of lung cancer and colorectal cancer, which are other highly prevalent malignancies, were also documented, but each was limited to a single case [ 24 , 25 ]. Among gynecological malignancies, there were two reported cases of coexistence with uterine corpus cancer, both of which were incidentally identified at the timing of initial treatment [ 26 , 27 ]. Reports of such difficulty in distinguishing coexistence from recurrence, as observed in the present case, are considered to be very rare, not only in gynecological malignancies but also in other malignancies. Namely, even if one were to suspect a false positive, it would be practically difficult to suspect FL because of the low pretest probability. Therefore, a pathological examination is necessary for an accurate diagnosis. Table 1. Literature review of concurrent follicular lymphoma and other malignancies Co-occurrence malignancies Initial or follow up Site of FL(G1) References Breast Initial SLN Hiraoka, et al. 2022 [ 28 ] Breast Initial AxLN Eto, et al. 2022 [ 29 ] Breast Initial Inguinal Saleem, et al. 2021 [ 18 ] Breast Initial AxLN Saleem, et al. 2021 [ 18 ] Breast Initial Left Breast Mirkheshi, et al. 2019 [ 30 ] Breast Follow up Retroperitoneal Papanastasopoulos, et al. 2015 [ 31 ] Breast Initial Inguinal LN Michalinos, et al. 2015 [ 32 ] Breast Initial SLN Laudenschlager, et al. 2010 [ 33 ] Breast Initial AxLN Cox, et al.2006 [ 34 ] Breast Initial AxLN Barranger, et al. 2005 [ 35 ] Colorectal Initial Mesenteric LN M.B, et al. 2005 [ 25 ] Lung Initial Upper abdomen Takahara, et al. 2024 [ 24 ] Uterine Corpus Initial Retroperitoneal Yoshino, et al. 2019 [ 27 ] Uterine Corpus Initial Retroperitoneal HB Lee, et al. 2011 [ 26 ] Uterine Corpus Follow up Retroperitoneal Present case Open in a new tab Initial or Follow up: The timing of finding of FL. At the timing of initial treatment (Initial) or during follow up (Follow up) SLN Sentinel lymph node, AxLN Axially lymph node. In this case, resection of all suspected lesions was difficult. Considering the high possibility of recurrence of malignancies other than corpus cancer, we decided to perform laparoscopic surgery to minimize physical damage as much as possible. We decided to resect only the areas in which the two abnormalities (size change and FDG accumulation) overlapped. The surrounding large vessels and ureter are good landmarks for the resection site. Since visible information is the only source of information in laparoscopies, large vessels and ureters with pulsation and peristalsis are good landmarks. In addition, although not used in this case, fluorescent materials such as indocyanine green injections and fluorescent ureteral stents can also be effective. In conclusion, we encountered a case in which FL was revealed by gradual enlargement of the pelvic lymph nodes during the follow-up of corpus cancer. FL can be considered when there is a discrepancy between the change in lymph node size and the degree of FDG accumulation. A pathological examination is essential for accurate diagnosis; however, care should be taken to minimize invasiveness as much as possible. Declarations Conflict of interest There are no conflicts of interest to be disclosed in this case report. Informed consent Informed consent was obtained from all participants included in this report. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Haruko Okamoto and Koji Yamanoi have equally contributed to the correspondence of this report. References 1. Nakai H, Higashi T, Kakuwa T et al (2024) Trends in gynecologic cancer in Japan: incidence from 1980 to 2019 and mortality from 1981 to 2021. Int J Clin Oncol 29:363–371. 10.1007/s10147-024-02473-8 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Yoshino K, Kurita T, Takahashi F et al (2023) Annual report of the committee on gynecologic oncology, the Japan society of obstetrics and gynecology: annual patient report for 2020 and annual treatment report for 2015. J Obstet Gynaecol Res 49:2584–2592. 10.1111/jog.15767 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Zhu Z, Zhou N, Yu S et al (2023) Successful treatment of concurrent follicular lymphoma and triple-negative