cholar ] 34. Cox J, Lunt L, Webb L (2006) Synchronous presentation of breast carcinoma and lymphoma in the axillary nodes. Breast 15:246–252. 10.1016/j.breast.2005.06.009 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 35. Barranger E, Marpeau O, Uzan S et al (2005) Axillary sentinel node involvement by breast cancer coexisting with B-cell follicular lymphoma in nonsentinel nodes. Breast J 11:227–228. 10.1111/j.1075-122X.2005.21697.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',10,'2025-10-13 06:59:51','2025-10-13 06:59:51');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1732,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11465112','SMARCA4-deficient uterine tumors in young women: response to immune checkpoint inhibitors.','Suzui R, Taki M, Kitamura S, Sunada M, Yamanoi K, Murakami R, Yamaguchi K, Hamanishi J, Minamiguchi S, Mandai M.','2024','sdus brg1 sdus sdus muts multikinase multikinase sdus s13691 sdus brg1 smarcb1 ini nonfermenting hypercalcemic sccoht inactivates smacra4 sdus sdus hyperchromatic rhabdoid smarca2 sdus omentectomy sdus pt1bpn1mx micrometastasis hyperchromatic rhabdoid smarca2 discohesive rhabdoid pt1bpn0mx muts sdus sdus smarcb1 sdus kolin sdus sdus sdus sdus sdus monotonous rhabdoid sdus sdus suzui tah bso pom cbdca gln59 ala1186fs kord kolin tah bso pom pmmr kolin tah bso pom 3426delcys 4759gly pmmr kolin 2554ala pmmr kolin psg8 kolin tah bso pom 582_587glycyscyscyscysthr tah bso dtx 190_616del h571glyfs baa tlh bso pom pazopanib kurokawa tah bso ifm tah bso pom omentectomy tlh cbdca dtx adriamycin ifm pmmr sdus pazopanib eribulin rhabdoid progressions sdus sdus 13691_2024_721_moesm1_esm 13691_2024_721_moesm2_esm 100μm 13691_2024_721_moesm3_esm file3 editage editage auguste deloger hypercalcemic sccoht kolin kihara amano matsubara smarca2 smarcb1 kolin baltay rhabdoid shinno alessi ricciuti spurr nonfermentable e133 e136 ramalingam croce mccluggage brg1 smarca2 brm smarcb1 ini1 rhabdoid kommoss tessier cloutier witkowski kord eppurath drammeh e00231 baa tripathy unfolding sdus kurokawa shimizuguchi blay cesne acse penebre smarca2 hypercalcemic kurashima kashiwagi shimomura heterochromatin zcaa005 13691_2024_721_moesm1_esm 13691_2024_721_moesm2_esm 100μm 13691_2024_721_moesm3_esm file3','Abstract SMARCA4-deficient tumors have been reported in various organs and are associated with a poor prognosis. SMARCA4-deficient undifferentiated uterine sarcoma (SDUS) was first described in 2018. Conversely, loss of SMARCA4 (BRG1) expression, as observed by immunostaining, has been observed in several cases of undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma. SDUS has considerable morphologic overlap with undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma, while there are differences in their clinicopathological features. Here, we present two cases of SMARCA4-deficient uterine tumors in patients in their 20 s: SDUS ( Case 1 ) and undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma without SMARCA4 nuclear expression ( Case 2 ). Using comprehensive genome profiling, we found that both cases had SMARCA4 mutations, with tumor mutation burdens of 0 and 68 Muts/Mb, respectively. Case 1 had multiple lung metastases 9 months after surgery. We treated the patients with combination of an immune checkpoint inhibitor (pembrolizumab) and a multikinase inhibitor (lenvatinib), and the response to the treatment was stable. This study presents the first report on the response to immune checkpoint inhibitor and multikinase inhibitor in SDUS. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1007/s13691-024-00721-2. Keywords: SDUS, Undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma, SMARCA4, ICIs Introduction SMARCA4-deficient tumors are rare and associated with poor prognosis. These tumors are characterized by the loss of SMARCA4 (BRG1) or SMARCB1 (INI-1) expression, components of the switch/sucrose nonfermenting (SWI/SNF) chromatin remodeling complex. SMARCA4-deficient tumors have been mostly reported in the thoracic regions with various phenotypes, such as sarcomas and carcinomas. In the gynecological tract, small cell carcinoma of the ovary, hypercalcemic type (SCCOHT), frequently inactivates germline and somatic mutations in SMARCA4 [ 1 ]. Several recent reports have shown that some uterine sarcomas and undifferentiated endometrial carcinomas share SMARCA4 inactivation [ 2 – 4 ]. We experienced two cases of SMACRA4-deficient uterine tumors in young women: uterine sarcoma (SMARCA4-deficient undifferentiated uterine sarcoma; SDUS) and uterine undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma. Herein, we present these cases and discuss their similarities and differences between the two cases. This is the first report on the therapeutic effect of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) on SDUS. Case reports Case 1 A 28-year-old nulliparous woman presented with an enlarged pelvic tumor and atypical genital bleeding that had persisted for 2 months. The patient had no significant medical or family history. Imaging studies revealed a uterine tumor 12 cm in size, with restricted diffusion, along with enlarged pelvic and paraaortic lymph nodes (Supplementary Fig. 1 ). Endometrial curettage revealed a solid growth of atypical hyperchromatic cells with notable mitotic activity, which was similar to rhabdoid tumor cells. Immunohistochemistry revealed a loss of nuclear SMARCA4 or SMARCA2 expression. The Ki-67 index was 50%, and no epithelial cells were observed. The preoperative pathological diagnosis was SDUS. Blood tests revealed elevated LDH (557 U/L; reference: 124–222 U/L) and CA125 (76.7 U/mL; reference: < 35 U/mL) levels. Three weeks after her initial presentation, she underwent a total abdominal hysterectomy, bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy, pelvic and paraaortic lymphadenectomy, and partial omentectomy. There were an enlarged uterus and enlarged pelvic lymph nodes without peritoneal metastasis. The ascites was negative for malignancy. The postoperative pathological diagnosis was SDUS, stage IIIC (pT1bpN1MX), with notable lymphovascular invasion and pelvic lymph node micrometastasis (Fig. 1 A–C and supplementary Fig. 2 ). The tumor was composed of sheets of small hyperchromatic cells without epithelial differentiation resembling rhabdoid tumor cells. Comprehensive genomic profiling revealed that the tumor had frameshift and nonsense mutations in SMARCA4 . No other mutations were detected. The tumor mutation burden (TMB) was 0 Mut/Mb, and the tumor was microsatellite stable. She received paclitaxel and carboplatin as adjuvant chemotherapy; however, two lung metastases recurred 9 months after surgery. We chose ICIs to treat recurrence because it has been reported that SMARCA4-deficient lung tumors respond to ICIs [ 5 – 7 ]. She received pembrolizumab (anti-PD-1 antibody) and lenvatinib (multi-targeted tyrosine kinase inhibitor) for 5 months, and the therapeutic effect was stable disease (SD) (Fig. 2 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Pathological findings of Case 1 and 2. (A–C) Case 1 , (D–F) Case 2 ; (A, D) hematoxylin–eosin (HE) staining, (B and E) SMARCA4 nuclear staining, (C and F) SMARCA2 staining. Scale bars: (A–F) 100 μm Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Therapeutic effect of pembrolizumab and lenvatinib for 5 months on two lung metastases in Case 1 Case 2 A 24-year-old nulliparous woman presented with atypical genital bleeding persisting for 3 months. When she was 9 years old, she had osteosarcoma and experienced two recurrences. The patient underwent multiple surgeries and chemotherapy. In addition, she had chemotherapy-induced amenorrhea and had received estrogen supplementation for 5 years. Imaging studies revealed a tumor with restricted diffusion in her uterus (Supplementary Fig. 3 A and B). Endometrial curettage revealed a pattern-less, sheet-like growth of discohesive cells with high mitotic activity resembling rhabdoid tumor cells and loss of SMARCA4. The preoperative diagnosis was undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma without SMARCA4 expression because epithelial membrane antigen (EMA), an epithelial cell marker, was expressed though the morphology was similar to Case 1 . Blood tests showed elevated levels of CA125 (79.6 U/mL; reference: < 35 U/mL), whereas LDH levels were within normal limits (152 U/L; reference: 124–222 U/L). Due to her refusal to treatment, the same surgical approach as in Case 1 was performed 5 months after her first visit; however, the tumor in Case 2 showed slower growth than Case 1 . Postoperative pathology confirmed undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma (SMARCA4-deficient), stage IB (pT1bpN0MX) (Fig. 1 D–F and supplementary Fig. 3 C). She received paclitaxel and carboplatin as adjuvant chemotherapy, and there was no recurrence for 13 months after surgery. Comprehensive genomic profiling revealed two splice-site and two nonsense mutations in SMARCA4 , and one missense mutation each in TP53 and BRAF . Copy number alterations were not observed. The TMB was 68 Muts/Mb, and the tumor was microsatellite stable. ICIs were recommended because of high TMB levels, and MEK inhibitors were recommended because of BRAF mutations. We are currently monitoring these patients closely. Discussion We experienced two cases of SMARCA4-deficient uterine tumors, SDUS and undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma. SDUS was first described in 2018 [ 4 ], which is extremely rare and aggressive tumor with significant lymphovascular invasion. Undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma are also uncommon and aggressive tumors. Approximately 20% of undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma show loss of SMARCA4 or SMARCB1 expression. SMARCA4 deficiency in undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma is not associated with a poor prognosis [ 8 ]. Since SDUS has considerable morphologic overlap with undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma, pathological findings alone cannot distinguish them [ 2 , 9 ]. Kolin et al. reported the clinicopathologic difference between SDUS and undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma [ 2 ]. Patients with SDUS are younger women (average age, 36 years) with a median survival of 9 months, whereas those with undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma are older women (average age, 61 years) with longer disease-specific survival, a median survival of which is 36 months. Regarding their molecular characteristics, SDUS shows only inactivating mutations in SMARCA4 , whereas undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma more frequently shows TP53 mutations and microsatellite instability as well. Case 1 only showed inactivating mutations in SMARCA4 and rapid postoperative recurrence. Although the patient in Case 2 was younger than patients with typical undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma, comprehensive genomic profiling indicated multiple genomic abnormalities, including TP53 mutation in Case 2 . The number of genomic abnormalities was high in the young patient, which may reflect her history of long-term treatment for osteosarcoma. Therefore, while sharing a similar morphology, Cases 1 and 2 had different genetic backgrounds, which may have led to different clinical outcomes. We have summarized the nine reported cases and the current case of SDUS in Table 1 [ 4 , 10 – 13 ]. All cases were pathologically diagnosed as SDUS based on the presence of monotonous proliferation of rhabdoid tumor cells with loss of SMARCA4 expression. Of the ten patients, four underwent simple total hysterectomy with retroperitoneal lymph node dissection and bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy, all showing lymphatic invasion and lymph node metastasis. Although the necessity and effect of pelvic and paraaortic lymphadenectomy in uterine sarcomas remain unclear because lymph node metastasis is uncommon in uterine sarcomas [ 14 ], lymphadenectomy for accurate staging should be considered in SDUS. Table 1. Case reports of SMARCA4-deficient undifferentiated uterine sarcoma (SDUS) Author age stage Primary treatment Adjuvant therapy Progress Alteration of SMARCA4 Microsatellite / MMR Suzui et al. (2024) 28 IIIC TAH/BSO/PeN/PAN/pOM PTX + CBDCA Recurrence 9 months after surgery p.Gln59*, p.Ala1186fs*30 MSS Kord et al. (2020) [ 10 ] 46 IIIC Chemotherapy No data available Progressive 3 months after starting chemotherapy No data available No data available Kolin et al. (2018) [ 4 ] 25 No data available TAH/BSO/PeN/pOM No data available DOD 7 months after surgery No data available pMMR Kolin et al. (2018) [ 4 ] 33 No data available TAH/BSO/pOM No data available DOD 9 months after surgery c.3426delCys c.4759Gly > Thr pMMR Kolin et al. (2018) [ 4 ] 34 No data available Not done Not done DOD 1 month after biopsy c.2554Ala > Thr pMMR Kolin et al. (2018) [ 4 ] 29 No data available Not done Not done DOD 4 month after presenting SMARCA4-PSG8 inversion Not done Kolin et al. (2018) [ 4 ] 58 No data available TAH/BSO/pOM/appendectomy Not done DOD 43 months after surgery c.582_587GlyCysCysCysCysThr > Gly Not done Gao et al. (2023) [ 11 ] 27 No data available TAH/BSO GEM + DTX Recurrence and BSC while 2nd line chemotherapy c.355 + 190_616del p.H571Glyfs MSS Baa et al. (2021) [ 12 ] 62 IIIC TLH/BSO/PeN/PAN/pOM/appendectomy pazopanib Progressive 1 month after chemotherapy No data available MSS Kurokawa et al. (2021) [ 13 ] 51 No data available TAH/BSO/PeN/PAN ADM + IFM Recurrence 6 months and DOD 12 months after surgery No data available No data available Open in a new tab TAH Total abdominal hysterectomy, BSO Bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy, PeN Pelvic lymphadenectomy, PAN Paraaortic lymphadenectomy, pOM partial omentectomy, TLH Total laparoscopic hysterectomy, PTX Paclitaxel, CBDCA Carboplatin, GEM Gemcitabine, DTX Docetaxel, ADM Adriamycin, IFM Ifosfamide, DOD Dead of disease, BSC Best supportive case, MMR Mismatch repair, MSS Microsatellite stable, pMMR MMR proficient An effective chemotherapy regimen for SDUS remains uncertain despite the shorter survival. Gemcitabine–paclitaxel and radiotherapy were reported to be effective, while gemcitabine–docetaxel, pazopanib, doxorubicin–ifosfamide and eribulin, which are used to treat uterine sarcoma, were reportedly ineffective [ 10 – 13 ]. SMARCA4-deficient thoracic tumors have a high tumor mutation burden and their treatment with ICIs have been reported to be effective [ 5 , 15 – 17 ]. In Case 1 , comprehensive genome profiling showed its microsatellite stability and 0 Mut/Mb of TMB. However, some SMARCA4-deficient thoracic tumors have been reported to be effective for ICIs. Therefore, we administered a combination of pembrolizumab and lenvatinib to treat the initial recurrence. Although the therapeutic effect was SD, it prevented worsening of the disease; therefore, it is considered effective to a certain extent. In a phase 2 clinical trial, basket trial, using pembrolizumab for rare sarcomas derived from all organs, 12 cases with SMARCA4-deficient sarcoma or malignant rhabdoid tumors were included [ 18 ]. Though the origin organs of these tumors were not described, three cases showed partial response. Median progression-free survival was 2.0 months (0.7 months to not reached) with ten deaths and nine progressions. It suggests that ICIs work in some cases. EZH2 and ATR inhibitors currently under development have the potential to treat tumors with SMARCA4 mutations [ 19 , 20 ]. Since effective treatments for SDUS remain underdeveloped, further studies are required. In conclusion, despite SMARCA4-deficient tumors share the same morphology, SDUS and undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma have different clinical outcomes and genetic backgrounds. It would be important to examine SMARCA4-deficient uterine tumors by comprehensive genome profiling to identify effective treatment methods. Supplementary Information Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. 13691_2024_721_MOESM1_ESM.pptx (1.1MB, pptx) Supplementary file1 Fig. 1 MRI findings in Case 1. (A) T2-weighted image, (B) contrasted-enhanced T1-weighted image, (C) diffusion weighted image and (D) apparent diffusion coefficient map. Arrow: the uterine tumor (PPTX 1097 KB) 13691_2024_721_MOESM2_ESM.pptx (12.5MB, pptx) Supplementary file2 Fig. 2 The pathological findings of Case 1. (A) The uterine tumor, (B) lymphatic invasion of the uterine tumor and (C and D) lymph node metastasis; (A) macroscopic image, (B and C) Hematoxylin-Eosin (HE) staining and (D) SMARCA4 nuclear staining. Scale bars: (B–D) 100μm (PPTX 12769 KB) 13691_2024_721_MOESM3_ESM.pptx (1.2MB, pptx) Supplementary file3 Fig. 3 MRI findings in Case 2. (A) T2-weighted image and (B) diffusion weighted image. Arrow: the uterine tumor. (C) macroscopic image (PPTX 1277 KB) Acknowledgements The authors thank the patient for their cooperation for allowing the publication of the manuscript and any accompanying images. We would like to thank Editage ( www.editage.com ) for English language editing. Funding No funding was obtained from the private or public sector for this research. Data availability The datasets used during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Conflict of interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest related to this manuscript. Ethical approval All procedures performed in studies involving human participants were in accordance with the ethical standards of the institutional and/or national research committee and with the 1964 Helsinki declaration and its later amendments or comparable ethical standards. For this type of study, formal consent is not required. Informed consent Informed consent was obtained from the participants included in the study. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Auguste A, Blanc-Durand F, Deloger M et al (2020) Small cell carcinoma of the ovary, hypercalcemic type (SCCOHT) beyond. Cells. 9:1496 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Kolin DL, Quick CM, Dong F et al (2020) SMARCA4-deficient uterine sarcoma and undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma are distinct clinicopathologic entities. Am J Surg Pathol 44:263–270 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Kihara A, Amano Y, Matsubara D et al (2021) Infrequent loss of SMARCA4, SMARCA2, and SMARCB1 expression in uterine mesenchymal tumors. Hum Pathol 116:12–21 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Kolin DL, Dong F, Baltay M et al (2018) SMARCA4-deficient undifferentiated uterine sarcoma (malignant rhabdoid tumor of the uterus): a clinicopathologic entity distinct from undifferentiated carcinoma. Mod Pathol 31:1442–1456 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Shinno Y, Yoshida A, Masuda K et al (2022) Efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in SMARCA4-deficient thoracic tumor. Clin Lung Cancer 23:386–392 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Alessi JV, Ricciuti B, Spurr LF et al (2021) SMARCA4 and Other SWItch/sucrose nonfermentable family genomic alterations in NSCLC: clinicopathologic characteristics and outcomes to immune checkpoint inhibition. J Thorac Oncol 16:1176–1187 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Zhou H, Shen J, Liu J et al (2020) Efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in SMARCA4-Mutant NSCLC. J Thorac Oncol 15:e133–e136 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Ramalingam P, Croce S, McCluggage WG (2017) Loss of expression of SMARCA4 (BRG1), SMARCA2 (BRM) and SMARCB1 (INI1) in undifferentiated carcinoma of the endometrium is not uncommon and is not always associated with rhabdoid morphology. Histopathology 70:359–366 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Kommoss FK, Tessier-Cloutier B, Witkowski L et al (2022) Cellular context determines DNA methylation profiles in SWI/SNF-deficient cancers of the gynecologic tract. J Pathol 257:140–145 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Kord A, Eppurath A, Drammeh H et al (2020) SMARCA4-deficient uterine sarcoma: A case report and a concise review. Case Rep Womens Health 27:e00231 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Gao J, Fan R, Chen D et al (2023) Pathological characteristics and immune microenvironment of SMARCA4-deficient undifferentiated uterine sarcoma. Diagn Pathol 18:62 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Baa AK, Rastogi S, Tripathy S et al (2021) Unfolding the rarity of SMARCA4 deficient uterine sarcoma (SDUS): A case report. Gynecol Oncol Rep 37:100788 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Kurokawa M, Shimizuguchi T, Ito K et al (2021) Notable Response of SMARCA4-deficient undifferentiated uterine sarcoma to palliative radiation therapy.