INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1748,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11406455','Neighborhood Food Access in Early Life and Trajectories of Child Body Mass Index and Obesity.','Aris IM, Wu AJ, Lin PD, Zhang M, Farid H, Hedderson MM, Zhu Y, Ferrara A, Chehab RF, Barrett ES, Carnell S, Camargo CA Jr, Chu SH, Mirzakhani H, Kelly RS, Comstock SS, Strakovsky RS, O''Connor TG, Ganiban JM, Dunlop AL, Dabelea D, Breton CV, Bastain TM, Farzan SF, Call CC, Hartert T, Snyder B, Santarossa S, Cassidy-Bushrow AE, O''Shea TM, McCormack LA, Karagas MR, McEvoy CT, Alshawabkeh A, Zimmerman E, Wright RJ, McCann M, Wright RO, Coull B, Amutah-Onukagha N, Hacker MR, James-Todd T, Oken E; ECHO Cohort Consortium.','2024','geocoded supermarket underresourced overlooking geocoded esri geocoded decennial fara supermarket fara fara recumbent implausible secondhand prepregnancy chained impute secondhand etables secondhand secondhand secondhand eresults supermarkets supermarket midchildhood obesogens obesogenic retailers subjecting fara eresults healthful incentivizing supermarkets pantries eresults acyclic lilv lilv ereferences jamapediatr e243459 jamapediatr e243459 jamapediatr e243459 odoms agm agurs glanz ajcnut wolfson m23 usda jenssen bouchelle mayne fiks e2021050123 aris hivert rifas shiman aris gundersen ziliak njai e161 pcd11 laraia s13668 bjerregaard angquist nejmoa1713231 twig yaniv nejmoa1503840 peerson achterberg grummer strawn porcherie preschoolers gossett e1406 e1413 jand gundersen lohman eisenmann garasky e854 e860 matheson varady varady killen bodor tester rosas whammy s13679 lillycrop burdge gluckman pbiomolbio aris fleisch oken s40471 blaisdell hanspal s41390 kress kwad071 lewinn caretta oken s41390 blaisdell arteaga spur kwae073 kwae073 aris ajcnut aris perng dabelea aris perng dabelea e2247957 usda aris perng dabelea e2146873 onis onyango borghi siyam siekmann kuczmarski rautava turta vahtera prepregnant fnut flanagin kuban kck boynton jarrett heeren supermarket e12937 forseth walkability s12966 curriero moodie swinburn jval puhl latner lobstein moodie peripuberty jadohealth radimer radimer industrialised phn2002385 deserts kladnicka bludovska plavinova mullerova obesogens biom12050680 genualdi beekman fluoroalkyl s00216 rummo heinonen e65 e73 e12908 epel physbeh nowson laraia vinikoor imler siega riz alegria jaac kobulsky sarwer e13204 calise ryder wingerter walkability dubowitz deserts deserts s11524 cureton reveh queralt sallis jerrett wolch 069x karter corbie deserts dc17 kelli samman tahhan deserts e010694 sobush keener recomm eresults acyclic lilv lilv ereferences jamapediatr e243459 jamapediatr e243459 jamapediatr e243459','Key Points Question Is neighborhood food access in early life associated with trajectories of child body mass index and obesity risk? Findings In this cohort study including 28 359 children from 55 cohorts, residence in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods during pregnancy or early childhood was associated with higher body mass index z score and higher risk of obesity and severe obesity from childhood to adolescence. These associations were robust to alternative definitions of low income and low food access and additional adjustment for prenatal characteristics associated with child obesity. Meaning Future studies should examine the effectiveness of investments in neighborhood resources to improve food access in preventing child obesity. This cohort study evaluates associations between neighborhood food access in early life and childhood body mass index and risk of obesity. Abstract Importance Limited access to healthy foods, resulting from residence in neighborhoods with low food access, is a public health concern. The contribution of this exposure in early life to child obesity remains uncertain. Objective To examine associations of neighborhood food access during pregnancy or early childhood with child body mass index (BMI) and obesity risk. Design, Setting, and Participants Data from cohorts participating in the US nationwide Environmental Influences on Child Health Outcomes consortium between January 1, 1994, and March 31, 2023, were used. Participant inclusion required a geocoded residential address in pregnancy (mean 32.4 gestational weeks) or early childhood (mean 4.3 years) and information on child BMI. Exposures Residence in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods, defined as low-income neighborhoods where the nearest supermarket is more than 0.5 miles for urban areas or more than 10 miles for rural areas. Main Outcomes and Measures BMI z score, obesity (age- and sex-specific BMI ≥95th percentile), and severe obesity (age- and sex-specific BMI ≥120% of the 95th percentile) from age 0 to 15 years. Results Of 28 359 children (55 cohorts; 14 657 [51.7%] male and 13 702 [48.3%] female; 590 [2.2%] American Indian, Alaska Native, Native Hawaiian, or Other Pacific Islander; 1430 [5.4%] Asian; 4034 [15.3%] Black; 17 730 [67.2%] White; and 2592 [9.8%] other [unspecified] or more than 1 race; 5754 [20.9%] Hispanic and 21 838 [79.1%] non-Hispanic) with neighborhood food access data, 23.2% resided in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods in pregnancy and 24.4% in early childhood. After adjusting for individual sociodemographic characteristics, residence in low-income, low–food access (vs non–low-income, low–food access) neighborhoods in pregnancy was associated with higher BMI z scores at ages 5 years (β, 0.07; 95% CI, 0.03-0.11), 10 years (β, 0.11; 95% CI, 0.06-0.17), and 15 years (β, 0.16; 95% CI, 0.07-0.24); higher obesity risk at 5 years (risk ratio [RR], 1.37; 95% CI, 1.21-1.55), 10 years (RR, 1.71; 95% CI, 1.37-2.12), and 15 years (RR, 2.08; 95% CI, 1.53-2.83); and higher severe obesity risk at 5 years (RR, 1.21; 95% CI, 0.95-1.53), 10 years (RR, 1.54; 95% CI, 1.20-1.99), and 15 years (RR, 1.92; 95% CI, 1.32-2.80). Findings were similar for residence in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods in early childhood. These associations were robust to alternative definitions of low income and low food access and additional adjustment for prenatal characteristics associated with child obesity. Conclusions Residence in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods in early life was associated with higher subsequent child BMI and higher risk of obesity and severe obesity. We encourage future studies to examine whether investments in neighborhood resources to improve food access in early life would prevent child obesity. Introduction Food insecurity, which occurs when individuals or households have limited access to adequate food because of financial constraints or residence in underresourced food environments, is a major public health issue. 1 , 2 The prevalence of food insecurity in US households with children younger than 18 years remains high and increased from 12.5% in 2021 to 17.3% in 2022. 3 Concurrently, the prevalence of childhood obesity, another major public health concern, has been steadily rising. 4 , 5 , 6 Food insecurity and obesity also coexist in many children and adolescents, 7 which is worrying, as both are linked with long-term health problems, including cardiovascular disease and diabetes. 8 , 9 , 10 , 11 , 12 , 13 Although the evidence in adults suggests that food insecurity may predispose individuals toward obesity, 14 , 15 the evidence in children remains equivocal. Some studies have shown associations between food insecurity and higher obesity risk in children, 16 , 17 , 18 while others have shown no associations 19 , 20 or associations in the opposite direction. 21 , 22 This inconsistency might be because most studies were cross-sectional, 23 which precludes understanding the temporal relationship between the 2 variables. Further, the few longitudinal studies of food insecurity and child obesity 24 have focused primarily on examining food insecurity in late childhood or adolescence, overlooking earlier life stages, such as pregnancy or early childhood, that are more developmentally plastic 25 and may be more sensitive in influencing long-term health outcomes. 26 Therefore, a better understanding of the relationship between food insecurity in early life and subsequent child obesity is crucial to develop preventive strategies that may mitigate future chronic conditions. To address these research gaps, we examined data from racially, ethnically, and geographically diverse mother-child pairs enrolled in prospective cohorts participating in the Environmental Influences on Child Health Outcomes (ECHO) consortium. 27 , 28 , 29 , 30 We sought to determine the extent to which neighborhood food access (primary aim) and caregiver food insecurity (secondary aim) during pregnancy and early childhood may contribute to childhood obesity risk. We hypothesized that children born to mothers residing in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods or experiencing food insecurity during pregnancy, or themselves having similar exposures in early childhood, would experience higher body mass index (BMI) and risk of obesity across childhood, independent of individual sociodemographic characteristics. Methods Study Population ECHO comprises extant cohorts of children across the US that had collected information on environmental exposures before age 5 years, including pregnancy, and assessed health outcomes across childhood. 27 , 28 , 29 Most ECHO cohorts started recruitment in prenatal obstetric clinics or at birth. 30 Recruitment of new participants and follow-up of existing cohort participants is ongoing (2023 to 2030). 31 , 32 , 33 For this analysis, we used data that were collected after January 1, 1994, and harmonized and shared on the ECHO-wide cohort data platform by March 31, 2023. We selected participants with information on child BMI and high-quality data on geocoded residential address (ie, either a point or specific street address) during pregnancy or early childhood. Pregnant participants, or the child’s parents or guardians, provided written informed consent for participation in the cohort of origin, and institutional review boards at each local study site or a central ECHO institutional review board approved the protocol. This study followed the Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology ( STROBE ) reporting guideline. Exposure Our primary exposure was residence in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods in pregnancy (mean 32.4 gestational weeks) or early childhood (mean age 4.3 years). Using ArcGIS geospatial software version 10.6 (Esri), the ECHO Data Analysis Center geocoded residential addresses obtained during pregnancy (year of residence 1994 to 2023) or early childhood (1999 to 2023), assigned a Census tract location to each address using decennial Census tract boundaries and linked the resultant Census tract location closest in time to the year of residence to Census tract–level food access data from the Food Access Research Atlas (FARA) in 2015 or 2019. 34 As detailed previously, 31 we defined low-income, low–food access neighborhoods as low-income Census tracts (federal poverty rate ≥20% or median family income ≤80% of the statewide median family income) with low food access (nearest supermarket >0.5 miles for urban or >10 miles for rural areas) in accordance with FARA guidelines. 34 We also considered other low-income, low–food access definitions in FARA and caregiver food insecurity as secondary exposures (eMethods in Supplement 1 ). Outcomes Each included cohort obtained at least 1 measure of child weight and simultaneous recumbent length (if younger than 2 years) or standing height (if 2 years or older) from birth to adolescence. 33 , 35 Sources of these measurements included in-person research visits, pediatric medical records, and mother or caregiver reports. We calculated BMI as weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared and derived age- and sex-specific z scores using the World Health Organization standards 36 (if younger than 2 years) or US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention reference 37 (if 2 years or older). We excluded biologically implausible values if the z score was less than −5 or greater than 5 for children younger than 2 years, or less than −4 or greater than 8 for children 2 years and older. 36 , 37 We defined obesity as age- and sex-specific BMI ≥95th percentile, and severe obesity as age- and sex-specific BMI ≥120% of the 95th percentile. 36 , 37 Covariates We obtained information on sociodemographic characteristics from maternal or caregiver reports and categorized them as follows: maternal age (in years) and education level during pregnancy (less than high school, high school diploma or equivalent, some college but no degree, or college degree and above), number of individuals living in the household (1-2, 3-4, or ≥5), insurance status (Medicaid, private, any other insurance, or no insurance), prenatal cigarette smoking or secondhand smoke exposure (yes or no), parity (0 or ≥1), maternal race (American Indian or Alaska Native, Asian, Black, Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander, White, multiple races, or other race), maternal ethnicity (Hispanic or non-Hispanic), and child’s sex (male or female) and birth year (before 2000, 2000-2010, or after 2010). Due to the small sample size, we combined mothers whose races were reported as American Indian or Alaska Native or as Native Hawaiian or Pacific Islander into a single category, and mothers of multiple races or other racial groups into a single category. Race and ethnicity data were reported because we considered those characteristics to be proxy measures of structural racism that can have implications for both residence in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods and lack of access to resources, which may promote unhealthy weight. We obtained information on prepregnancy BMI, total gestational weight gain, gestational diabetes (yes or no), and gestational hypertension or preeclampsia (yes or no) from medical records. We defined residency as urban if the Census tract contained more than 2500 people and rural if 2500 people or fewer. We selected these covariates a priori based on prior publications examining associations between food insecurity and health outcomes (eFigure 1 in Supplement 1 ). 16 , 17 , 18 , 19 , 20 , 21 , 22 , 23 , 31 Statistical Analyses We used linear mixed-effects models to examine associations of low-income, low–food access residency during pregnancy or early childhood with repeated BMI z scores, and generalized linear mixed-effects models with a logit link for repeated obesity or severe obesity outcomes. We fitted separate models for low-income, low–food access exposure at each period. Each model included the low-income, low–food access exposure, child age as a linear term (as prior studies have described linear trajectories of BMI z score 38 , 39 ), interaction of low-income, low–food access with child age to capture the extent to which the association may change across age, and sociodemographic characteristics as covariates. In accordance with our prior work on low-income, low–food access neighborhoods, 31 we did not adjust for race and ethnicity because we viewed them as societal constructs, rather than deterministic biological causes of disease risk. 40 , 41 , 42 , 43 We included child-level random intercepts and slopes for (linear) age to account for within-child correlation between repeated outcomes, cohort-level random intercepts to account for clustering of children from the same cohort, and Census tract–level random intercepts to account for clustering of children residing within the same neighborhood. We also explored whether changes to residence in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods between pregnancy and early childhood may be differentially associated with outcomes, by including separate terms for low income and low food access in pregnancy and early childhood as well as an interaction term between both variables in the model. To ensure the exposure preceded the outcome, we modeled repeated BMI z scores or obesity measures obtained at or after the residential address date for low-income, low–food access exposure. We used these models to estimate mean predicted BMI z score and probabilities of obesity and severe obesity over time for low-income, low–food access status, holding covariates constant at their mean values and setting the random effects to zero. The estimated trajectories can thus be interpreted as the trajectory of BMI z score, obesity, or severe obesity for the average child in the average cohort in the average Census tract in a low-income, low–food access or non–low-income, low–food access neighborhood. We also estimated adjusted mean differences in predicted BMI z score and risk ratios (RRs) of obesity or severe obesity (and corresponding 95% CIs) at ages 5, 10, and 15 years for low-income, low–food access (vs non–low-income, low–food access) neighborhoods. We conducted several secondary analyses to test robustness of our associations for low-income, low–food access neighborhoods and examine associations of caregiver food insecurity with child obesity (eMethods in Supplement 1 ). We used multiple imputation by chained equations to impute missing covariate data (eTable 1 in Supplement 1 ) under the missing-at-random assumption. 44 We generated 50 imputed datasets for all participants in the analytic sample. The imputation model included the exposure, outcome, and covariates under study. We pooled the imputation-specific results into an aggregate model using the pool function in R version 4.2.2 (R Foundation). When interpreting findings, we focused on the direction, strength, and precision of the estimates and used 2-sided α = .05 to assess statistical significance. Results Of 69 ECHO cohorts, we included 28 359 children (14 657 [51.7%] male and 13 702 [48.3%] female) from 55 cohorts with neighborhood food access data during pregnancy or early childhood ( Figure 1 ). Of the included individuals, 590 (2.2%) identified as American Indian, Alaska Native, Native Hawaiian, or Other Pacific Islander; 1430 (5.4%) as Asian; 4034 (15.3%) as Black; 17 730 (67.2%) as White; and 2592 (9.8%) as other (unspecified) or more than 1 race; 5754 (20.9%) identified as Hispanic and 21 838 (79.1%) as non-Hispanic. A total of 9054 of 17 620 (51.4%) had at least a college degree, and 5882 of 8724 (67.4%) had private insurance during pregnancy (eTable 1 in Supplement 1 ). Additionally, 5800 of 24 957 (23.2%) resided in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods during pregnancy and 4923 of 20 175 (24.4%) in early childhood (eTable 2 in Supplement 1 ). Participants residing in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods in pregnancy or early childhood were more likely to identify as Black or Hispanic compared with other race and ethnicity groups and less likely to have a college degree or private insurance ( Table ). The proportions of children with obesity and severe obesity were 9% and 0.5% at 0 to less than 2 years, 12.5% and 2.1% at 2 to less than 5 years, 15.5% and 5.3% at 5 to less than 10 years, and 20.6% and 8.6% at 10 to 15 years, respectively. From 0 to 15 years, 25.7% of children had 1 BMI measure, 16.0% had 2, 11.1% had 3, and 47.0% had 4 or more. Figure 1. Location of Participating Study Cohorts and Flow Diagram of Analytic Sample. Open in a new tab BMI indicates body mass index; ECHO, Environmental Influences on Child Health Outcomes cohort. Table. Participant Characteristics According to Neighborhood Food Access in Pregnancy and Early Childhood. Characteristic Neighborhood food access, % a In pregnancy In early childhood Non-LILA (n = 19 157 [76.8%]) LILA (n = 5800 [23.2%]) Non-LILA (n = 15 252 [75.6%]) LILA (n = 4923 [24.4%]) Maternal ethnicity b Hispanic 19.5 24.0 16.9 24.2 Non-Hispanic 80.5 76.0 83.1 75.8 Maternal race b American Indian, Alaska Native, Native Hawaiian, or Other Pacific Islander c 1.9 2.3 1.7 2.4 Asian 7.0 4.3 6.7 4.0 Black 9.4 32.5 10.1 34.8 Other (unspecified) or ≥1 race d 9.9 10.8 8.3