and 3 ). Specifically, the mean BMI z score difference in children who resided in low-income, low–food access (vs non–low-income, low–food access) neighborhoods during pregnancy was 0.07 (95% CI, 0.03-0.11) at age 5 years, 0.11 (95% CI, 0.06-0.17) at 10 years, and 0.16 (95% CI, 0.07-0.24) at 15 years, while the RRs of obesity and severe obesity were 1.37 (95% CI, 1.21-1.55) and 1.21 (95% CI, 0.95-1.53) at 5 years, 1.71 (95% CI, 1.37-2.12) and 1.54 (95% CI, 1.20-1.99) at 10 years, and 2.08 (95% CI, 1.53-2.83) and 1.92 (95% CI, 1.32-2.80) at 15 years. Findings were similar for residence in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods in early childhood (eTables 3-4 in Supplement 1 ). Changes to residence in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods between pregnancy and early childhood were associated with higher BMI z score and probability of obesity and severe obesity ( Figure 4 ), but 95% CIs for differences included the null (eTable 5 in Supplement 1 ). However, those who resided in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods in both pregnancy and early childhood had the highest risk of obesity at 10 and 15 years (eTable 5 in Supplement 1 ). Figure 2. Child Body Mass Index (BMI) z Score and Probability of BMI ≥95th Percentile by Neighborhood. Open in a new tab A, n = 24 957. B, n = 20 175. Adjusted for mother’s age, educational level, and insurance during pregnancy; number of individuals in household; prenatal cigarette smoking; prenatal secondhand smoke exposure; parity; and child’s birth year. LILA indicates low-income, low–food access. Figure 3. Probability of Severe Obesity by Neighborhood. Open in a new tab A, n = 24 957. B, n = 20 175. Adjusted for mother’s age, educational level, and insurance during pregnancy; number of individuals in household; prenatal cigarette smoking; prenatal secondhand smoke exposure; parity; and child’s birth year. LILA indicates low-income, low–food access. Figure 4. Child Body Mass Index (BMI) z Score, Probability of BMI ≥95th Percentile, and Severe Obesity by Changes in Residence . Open in a new tab In this figure, n = 17 780; 53 cohorts. Adjusted for mother’s age, educational level, and insurance during pregnancy; number of individuals in household; prenatal cigarette smoking; prenatal secondhand smoke exposure; parity; and child’s birth year. LILA indicates low-income, low–food access. These associations were robust to alternative low-income, low–food access definitions and additional adjustment for prenatal characteristics associated with child obesity. In additional secondary analyses, caregiver food insecurity in early childhood, but not in pregnancy, was associated with higher risk of obesity in childhood and adolescence, albeit with imprecise estimates (eResults in Supplement 1 ). Discussion In this cohort study, we observed that residence in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods in early life was associated with higher BMI z score and higher risk of obesity and severe obesity from childhood to adolescence, independent of individual sociodemographic characteristics. Our findings generally align with results from prior studies. A recent systematic review 45 identified 11 studies from the US and Canada that showed associations between low access to supermarkets in the neighborhood and higher risk of child obesity, although definitions for supermarket access differed across all studies. In Kansas, Jiang et al 46 reported that children who resided in low-income, low–food access (vs non–low-income, low–food access) neighborhoods in midchildhood in 2012 to 2014 had higher subsequent BMI z scores in 2017 to 2019. In Baltimore, Johnson et al 47 found that in a predominantly low-income Black and Hispanic population, children living in neighborhoods with the lowest availability of stores accepting WIC (Special Supplemental Nutrition Program for Women, Infants and Children) benefits—an indicator of poor access to healthy foods—showed more rapid gain in BMI z score over time compared to their peers living in neighborhoods with the highest availability of stores accepting WIC. However, these studies were mostly cross-sectional, 45 which subjects the findings to reverse causality, or had a short follow-up period, which limits understanding of longer-term effects. 46 Also, they were conducted only within a single US city or state, 46 , 47 which reduces generalizability to the US population, and largely examined neighborhood food access during childhood only, 45 , 46 , 47 which limits our understanding of associations at earlier life stages such as pregnancy, a developmentally sensitive period that lays the foundation for long-term health. 26 By directly addressing these key research gaps, our study contributes novel evidence to the growing body of literature linking neighborhood food access in early life with subsequent child obesity. Our results showed that residence in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods in pregnancy or early childhood was associated with a more than 50% higher obesity risk in childhood and adolescence. To put these findings in perspective, we previously showed in ECHO that residence in the lowest opportunity neighborhoods in early life was associated with an approximately 80% higher obesity risk at similar life stages. 33 The clinical benefits of reducing childhood obesity are well known—it can lead to higher quality of life, 48 lower likelihood of depression, 49 and higher economic productivity later in life. 50 We also noted that the association was strongest for those who resided in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods in both pregnancy and early childhood even after accounting for family sociodemographic characteristics, likely reflecting cumulative exposure to resource deprivation over time that operated jointly to negatively affect outcomes. 51 These findings suggest that pregnancy and early childhood are both important windows of exposure with respect to neighborhood food access, and that residence in neighborhoods with better food access during these life stages might constitute an important resilience factor that may prevent development of child obesity. Our secondary findings for caregiver food insecurity should be interpreted cautiously; they could be explained by smaller sample size for those with information on food insecurity and obesity (eTable 1 in Supplement 1 ), which limits statistical power and precision. Further, only a subset of ECHO cohorts with more underrepresented racial and ethnic minority populations and lower income provided information on caregiver food insecurity, thus limiting generalizability of the findings. We also ascertained information on caregiver food insecurity from only a single question (eMethods in Supplement 1 ), which may be less sensitive than other surveys 52 that assess food insecurity more comprehensively and may exclude those with less severe food insecurity. Several potential mechanisms may underlie our observations. Low-income, low–food access neighborhoods might have greater density of smaller convenience stores and fast food outlets, 53 which implies greater access to and increased consumption of highly processed and packaged foods containing obesogens 54 , 55 often sold in these stores. Thus, greater access to these stores could impair the diet quality of pregnant individuals and children, 56 which may subsequently affect child obesity. 57 Individuals residing in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods, including children, may also face higher levels of stress due to the financial and emotional pressures of food insecurity, poor housing, adverse childhood events, and neighborhood crime, which may lead to weight gain through stress-induced metabolic changes. 58 , 59 , 60 , 61 , 62 Low-income, low–food access neighborhoods could also reflect disadvantaged neighborhood conditions with poorer built environment characteristics that discourage physical activity 63 , 64 or higher exposure to other environmental stressors 65 that can affect child health outcomes. Hence, beyond affecting diet quality of children, residence in low-income, low–food access neighborhoods may negatively affect child obesity through reduced physical activity 66 or increased exposure to obesogenic stressors including environmental pollutants. 67 While these questions are beyond the scope of the current study, future studies in ECHO or other settings could be done to explore these potential mechanisms. Strengths and Limitations Strengths of our study include the large sample size for neighborhood food access and wide range of covariates. We used low-income, low–food access indices that have been validated for other key health outcomes. 68 , 69 We also controlled for individual-level factors that may influence residential selection. This study also has several limitations. First, we used Census tracts as a marker of exposure, which may not capture the relevant areas where participants spend most of their time. 32 , 33 Further, because of a lack of comparable data, the neighborhood food access measure used in this study did not include other neighborhood-level food environment characteristics (eg, density of food retailers) or social attributes (eg, neighborhood crime) that might affect child obesity, thus subjecting our findings to residual confounding. Second, certain covariates had a substantial percentage of missing data, which may have impacted our findings. However, we used flexible multiple imputation techniques that reduce bias and likelihood of spurious results. Third, our study included child anthropometric measures from mother or caregiver reports, which may be subject to measurement error. However, we believe our findings are not substantially affected by this issue, as less than 10% of the measures were obtained from mother or caregiver reports. Fourth, our findings may not be generalizable to other populations from different countries, as all study participants were from the US. Fifth, we used FARA information for 2015 and 2019, which may be misclassified for residential addresses obtained during 1990s or 2000s. However, results for low-income, low–food access restricted to residential addresses obtained during or after the year 2014 were similar to our main findings (eResults in Supplement 1 ). Conclusions The results of this large nationwide cohort study suggest that residence in low-income-low-food-access neighborhoods during early life is associated with higher BMI z score and higher risk of obesity and severe obesity from childhood to adolescence. Given the long-term sequelae associated with child obesity, 12 , 13 our findings support a focus on investments or strategies to improve healthful food access in early life, such as incentivizing new supermarkets in existing low-income, low–food access neighborhoods, providing healthy-choice pantries, and improving access to healthier food choices in small retail corner and convenience stores. 70 More research is warranted to investigate whether such strategies would be effective in preventing child obesity. Supplement 1. eMethods. Secondary exposures and secondary analyses eResults. Secondary analyses, and Caregiver food insecurity and trajectories of child BMI z-score and obesity eTable 1. Participant and family characteristics eTable 2. Neighborhood characteristics among participants in the analytic samples eTable 3. Association of residence in low-income-low-food-access (LILA) neighborhoods in pregnancy or early childhood with body mass index z-score at ages 5, 10, and 15 years eTable 4. Association of residence in low-income-low-food-access (LILA) neighborhoods in pregnancy or early childhood with risk of obesity or severe obesity at ages 5, 10, and 15 years eTable 5. Secondary analyses for the association of change in residence in low-income-low-food-access (LILA) neighborhoods in pregnancy and early childhood (n=17,780; 53 cohorts) with body mass index (BMI) z-score and risk of obesity and severe obesity at ages 5, 10, a