f caregiver food insecurity in early childhood with child body mass index (BMI) z-score (A) and risk of obesity (B) at age 10 years eFigure 16. Association of caregiver food insecurity in pregnancy or early childhood with child body mass index (BMI) z-score and risk of obesity at ages 5, 10, and 15 years according to residence in low-income-low-food-access (LILA) neighborhoods. All effect estimates are referenced to residence in non-LILA neighborhoods eReferences jamapediatr-e243459-s001.pdf (2MB, pdf) Supplement 2. ECHO Cohort Consortium members jamapediatr-e243459-s002.pdf (212.7KB, pdf) Supplement 3. Data sharing statement jamapediatr-e243459-s003.pdf (16.2KB, pdf)',46,'2025-10-13 07:03:31','2025-10-13 07:03:31');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1749,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12432784','Pulmonary metastasis risk and overall survival score in choriocarcinoma: a novel nomogram-based risk assessment system.','Li W, He Q, Huang Q, Deng Z.','2025','dcas dca choriocarcinomas ngcc ngcc seminomatous 30s distally nomograms amegroups linked_databases psite psite psite psite psite psite psite psite psite dca psite tpr penile totipotent hypodifferentiated actinomycin actinomycin actinomycin spt spts ngcc ngcc stratifies multiagent p97 vcp bep coif 323kb amegroups zigong zgyky22kf008 amegroups coif mangla kanikaram camilleri calleja aguis s12957 cabrero toriz paner 0b013e3182a2926e extragonadal cmar s175948 mgct byun tumori mangili s109 balachandran gonen nomograms e173 sindhu noor mansoor jpma ates altundag gullu buon devos willemse sterckx s1015 khanfir kaffel gouiaa stomatol stomax picazo ferrera servin gtd bpobgyn androl s12957 viggiano kaabia meddeb rhim almd kdn novelties guber zografos schalenbourg wildi runge crevier carret ghir sisti kanninen asciutti gudipudi hartzfeld montemarano shinoda yano neon paiva ghorani bogani coquard mutch desdicioglu yavuz p97 vcp autophagosome s11033 soper cianci tozzi coexistent prenat bolze schoenen margaillan','Abstract Background At present, the risk of developing pulmonary metastasis and prognostic factors for choriocarcinoma (CC) remain unclear. This study aimed to investigate the independent risk factors and prognostic factors for pulmonary metastasis of CC and to construct a prognostic prediction model. Methods We retrieved data on patients diagnosed with CC between 2010 and 2019 from the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results (SEER) database. Univariate and multivariate logistic regressions were used to identify independent risk factors for developing pulmonary metastases in CC. Then, univariate and multivariate COX regression analyses were used to identify independent risk factors affecting the prognosis of patients with CC. Finally, we constructed a predictive nomogram and assessed the efficacy of the nomogram by receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves, calibration curves, and decision analysis curves (DCAs). Results Independent risk factors for developing pulmonary metastases in CC patients were gender and tissue type. Independent risk factors for the prognosis of CC patients were age, marriage, primary location, liver metastases, lung metastases, and surgical intervention. The results of ROC curves, calibration curves, and DCA in the training and validation groups confirmed that the nomogram could accurately predict the prognosis of CC patients. Conclusions Patients with CC are more likely to be young, have a more primary male genital system, have a poor prognosis, and are most likely to be complicated by pulmonary metastasis at initial diagnosis. A novel prediction model to predict the prognosis of CC patients has been constructed to personalize and guide clinical decision-making. Keywords: Choriocarcinoma (CC); neoplasm metastasis; nomogram; prognostic factors; Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results (SEER) Highlight box. Key findings • Choriocarcinomas (CC) originating in different locations have different prognoses. Patients with primary placenta have a better prognosis. In this study, we developed a new prognostic nomogram for quantitatively assessing the overall survival (OS) rate of patients with CC to aid clinical decision-making. What is known and what is new? • CC, predominantly arising from hydatidiform moles (approximately 50% of cases), is characterized by its high malignancy potential, aggressive biological behavior with early hematogenous dissemination, and pulmonary metastases as the most common metastatic manifestation. Chemotherapy serves as the cornerstone of therapeutic management, underscoring the critical importance of early intervention to optimize clinical outcomes. • CC does not occur only in women. Male patients with CC combined with other types of germ cells are more likely to develop pulmonary metastases. Elderly patients with primary male genital CC have a poor prognosis, patients with primary placenta have a better prognosis, and surgical intervention improves prognosis, whereas chemotherapy and radiotherapy have no significant effect on prognosis. What is the implication, and what should change now? • CC does not occur only in the female reproductive system. This study found that the most common site of origin of CC is the male reproductive system (testes). And CC originating in the male reproductive system has a worse prognosis than that originating in the female placenta. In addition, a novel predictive model was constructed to predict the OS rate of CC, which is of great significance for clinical decision-making. Introduction Choriocarcinoma (CC) is a malignant tumor originating from embryonic trophoblast cells, which can be divided into gestational CC (GCC) and non-gestational CC (NGCC) ( 1 ). GCC is overwhelmingly associated with female pregnancy and is often secondary to hydatidiform mole ( 2 ). The occurrence of tubal pregnancy may lead to tubal pregnancy CC with an incidence of 1.5/1,000,000 ( 3 ). NGCC can also occur in men, where it is usually considered a non-seminomatous germ cell tumor, accounting for 0.6% of all germ cell tumors in men ( 4 ), with a median onset age at about 30s ( 5 , 6 ). CC is highly malignant and susceptible to distant metastases that can spread distally to the lung, liver, and central nervous system. However, gastrointestinal tract metastasis occurs in less than 5% of patients and is associated with a poor prognosis ( 7 , 8 ). In conclusion, CC has a low incidence, a high metastatic rate, a poor prognosis, and a high susceptibility to lung metastasis at an early stage. Furthermore, to the best of our knowledge, the risk factors for developing lung metastasis in CC are currently unclear and there is a lack of prognostic studies with large samples of this population. The Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results (SEER) database is an open access tumor database in the US. It is rich in content and provides a large, nationally representative sample for study. Nomogram, because of its visualization and flexibility, has recently been widely used to predict the prognosis of malignant tumors ( 9 , 10 ). Therefore, in this study, we explored the risk factors for the development of pulmonary metastases and prognostic factors of CC patients with subjects from the SEER database. Then, we developed predictive nomograms to quantitatively assess the survival rate of patients and guide clinical decision-making. In addition, we also verified the model internally and externally. We present this article in accordance with the TRIPOD reporting checklist (available at https://tcr.amegroups.com/article/view/10.21037/tcr-2025-206/rc ). Methods Data source The SEER database ( https://seer.cancer.gov/data-software/linked_databases.html ) consists of 18 population-based cancer registries covering 30% of the US population. It is an open oncology database that includes patient basic and survival information. It allows all healthcare workers to use, without the need to obtain institutional consent, and scientific researchers can use it free of charge. We extracted the data of CC patients from the database between 2010 and 2019. The study was conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki and its subsequent amendments. Patients Patient screening criteria—(I) inclusion criteria: International Classification of Diseases for Oncology (ICD-O)-3 Hist/Behav (9100/3, 9101/3); definite pathological diagnosis. (II) Exclusion criteria: patients diagnosed with death certificate. Overall survival (OS) was the primary study outcome. Information on age at diagnosis, gender, race, marriage, primary location, tissue type, surgery, chemotherapy, radiotherapy, etc., was collected. Finally, a total of 943 patients with CC were included in this study. They were randomized in a 7:3 ratio into a training group of 660 (70%) and a validation group of 283 (30%) ( Figure 1 ). This model was validated through two groups, the training group as well as the validation group. Figure 1. Open in a new tab The patients screening process. ICD-O, International Classification of Diseases for Oncology; SEER, Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results. Statistical analysis R software (version 4.2.1) was used for statistical analyses. Meanwhile, P<0.05 (bilateral) was considered statistically significant. First, the independent risk factors for the development of pulmonary metastases were clarified in all patients by using univariate and multivariate logistic regression analysis. The Chi-squared test was used to compare the distribution of variables in the training and validation groups. Then, univariate, and multivariate COX regression analyses were used to stepwise identify independent prognostic factors. Based on independent prognostic factors, we created a new predictive nomogram to predict OS rates at 1, 3, and 5 years in patients, and survival curves were constructed using Kaplan-Meier analysis to determine the associated median OS (mOS). In addition, time-dependent receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves were generated, and the corresponding time-dependent area under the curve (AUC) was applied for discrimination. Results Basic characteristics of patients with CC pulmonary metastases A total of 943 patients with a precise pathological diagnosis of CC were included, and 400 (42.4%) had pulmonary metastases. The highest proportion of those with pulmonary metastases was in young people (<45 years, 84.3%), with a male-to-female ratio of approximately 2:1. The rate of pulmonary metastases varied by primary location. The highest rate of pulmonary metastases originated in the male reproductive system, followed by the placenta ( Table 1 ). Table 1. Basic characteristics of patients with choriocarcinoma pulmonary metastases. Variables Lung metastasis Overall (N=943) No/unknown (N=543) Yes (N=400) Age (years) <45 462 (85.1) 337 (84.3) 799 (84.7) 45–64 67 (12.3) 53 (13.3) 120 (12.7) ≥65 14 (2.6) 10 (2.5) 24 (2.5) Sex Female 258 (47.5) 133 (33.3) 391 (41.5) Male 285 (52.5) 267 (66.8) 552 (58.5) Race Black 60 (11.0) 35 (8.8) 95 (10.1) White 406 (74.8) 319 (79.8) 725 (76.9) Other 77 (14.2) 46 (11.5) 123 (13.0) Marital Married 210 (38.7) 145 (36.3) 355 (37.6) Other 333 (61.3) 255 (63.8) 588 (62.4) pSite Female Rep 101 (18.6) 35 (8.8) 136 (14.4) Male Rep 247 (45.5) 235 (58.8) 482 (51.1) Placenta 134 (24.7) 91 (22.8) 225 (23.9) Other 61 (11.2) 39 (9.8) 100 (10.6) Histologic type 9100 321 (59.1) 288 (72.0) 609 (64.6) 9101 222 (40.9) 112 (28.0) 334 (35.4) Open in a new tab Data are presented as n (%). 9100, choriocarcinoma; 9101, choriocarcinoma combined with other germ cell elements; female Rep, female reproductive system; male Rep, male reproductive system; pSite, primary site. Risk factors for patients with CC pulmonary metastases We used univariate logistic regression analysis to investigate the occurrence of lung metastasis in patients, showing that gender, primary location and histologic type were correlated with lung metastasis. Then, multivariate logistic regression analysis results showed that gender and histological type were independent risk factors for pulmonary metastasis, indicating that men and CC patients combined with other types of germ cells were prone to pulmonary metastasis ( Table 2 ). Table 2. Risk factors for patients with choriocarcinoma pulmonary metastases. Variables Univariate analysis Multivariate analysis OR (95% CI) P OR (95% CI) P Age (years) <45 Reference 45–64 1.084 (0.735–1.594) 0.96 ≥65 0.979 (0.417–2.215) 0.68 Sex Female Reference Reference Male 1.817 (1.392–2.379) <0.001 3.017 (1.177–8.516) 0.03 Race Black Reference White 1.347 (0.871–2.112) 0.19 Other 1.024 (0.589–1.788) 0.93 Marital Married Reference Other 1.109 (0.849–1.450) 0.45 pSite Female Rep Reference Reference Male 