in clinical practice. Figure 4. Open in a new tab A prognostic nomogram to predict the 1-, 3-, and 5-year overall survival in choriocarcinoma patients. *, P<0.05, significant; **, P<0.01, highly significant; ***, P<0.001, extremely significant. Female Rep, female reproductive system; male Rep, male reproductive system; mo., months; Liver, liver metastases; Lung, lung metastases; Pr, probability; pSite, primary site. Figure 5. Open in a new tab Time-dependent receiver operating characteristic curve analysis of the nomogram for the 1, 3, and 5 years in the training set (A) and the validation set (B). AUC, area under the curve; FPR, false positive rate; TPR, true positive rate. Figure 6. Open in a new tab The calibration curves for predictions of overall survival in the training set (A-C) and in the validation set (D-F) at 1, 3, and 5 years. Figure 7. Open in a new tab The decision curve analysis of nomogram in the training set (A-C) and in the validation set (D-F) at 1, 3, and 5 years. Discussion This study is the first to investigate the risk factors for lung metastasis and prognosis of CC based on SEER database. We constructed a novel nomogram to quantify the prognostic factors which can individualize the assessment of prognosis. Moreover, we verified that the model had good efficacy in three dimensions: discriminative ability, predictive accuracy, and clinical practice efficacy. According to the International Society for the Study of Trophoblastic Diseases, distant metastases in CC patients mostly occurred in the lungs, liver and brain, which is the same as our findings ( 8 ). We found that people under 45 years of reproductive age (84.7%) were most likely to develop lung metastases, followed by liver, brain and bone metastases. In addition, this study shows that older patients with primary male genitalia and with metastases at other organs (such as lung and liver metastases) had a poorer prognosis. Among these independent prognostic factors, the significant factors were age and primary site. Based on the statistically significant factors identified above, we developed a prognostic nomogram to estimate 1-, 3-, and 5-year OS rates. This tool provides actionable guidance for clinical risk stratification and personalized intervention strategies. As shown in Figure 4 , a patient <45 years old, with unspecified marital status, with the primary male genital system, without pulmonary and hepatic metastases, and undergoing surgical intervention, could calculate a score of approximately 220 by nomogram, with corresponding OS rates of approximately 94%, 91%, and 90% at 1, 3, and 5 years, respectively. This finding indicates that young males with primary CC of the genital system (e.g., testicular or penile origin) and no distant metastasis achieve a 5-year OS rate of 90% following complete surgical resection of the primary lesion. CC usually originates in the uterus, but can also originate from other sites such as the testes, stomach, lungs, and intracranial ( 11 - 15 ). Interestingly, the most common site of origin of CC found in this study was the male reproductive system (testes) (51.1%), followed by the placenta (23.9%). This may be related to our increased awareness of the disease and advances in medical care. It is currently believed that CC is a malignant epithelial cell tumor with rapid growth and high metastatic potential that can be derived from trophoblast or totipotent cells ( 16 ). There are non-pregnant CC and GCC. When CC is present as a component of germ cell tumors or associated with hypodifferentiated carcinoma with somatic mutations, it is called non-pregnant CC ( 17 ). CC originating in pregnancy, called GCC, occurs in approximately 25% of its cases after miscarriage or tubal pregnancy, 25% associated with full-term pregnancy or preterm delivery, and the remaining 50% caused by hydatidiform mole, but only 2–3% of hydatidiform moles progress to CC ( 18 ). Currently, chemotherapy is one of the most important modalities for the treating CC. However, the present study showed no significant prognostic benefit of chemotherapy for CC. On the one hand, it may be because most of the CC in this study originated in the male reproductive system, and this type of CC is not sensitive to chemotherapy. Li et al. also showed that chemotherapy was not an independent prognostic factor for OS and cancer-specific survival (CSS) in testicular CC ( 19 ). On the other hand, it may also be related to the fact that we did not study the specific chemotherapy regimen and start time. It is now recognized that most patients with low-risk CC receive single-agent chemotherapy (e.g., actinomycin-D, etoposide, etc.), whereas high-risk patients receive combination chemotherapy, with specific regimens including EMA-CO (etoposide, methotrexate, actinomycin-D, cyclophosphamide, and vincristine) and MAC (methotrexate, actinomycin-D, cyclophosphamide), etc. ( 20 ). There is some controversy regarding the prognostic factors of patients with CC. Most CC have metastases at initial diagnosis, and survival remains low even after treatment ( 19 ), but some investigators consider CC the most curable gynecologic cancer with OS rates between 80–100% ( 21 - 23 ). Older and advanced tumor staging suggests a poor prognosis, while surgery improves the prognosis, and chemotherapy is not relevant for better CSS and OS ( 19 ). Another study ( 22 ) showed that chemotherapy improved the prognosis of CC, with survival rates increasing from 19% to 90%. Most of them are small-sample single-center studies. In contrast, the present large-sample multicenter study found that surgical intervention and primary placenta had a significant prognostic benefit, whereas chemotherapy and radiotherapy had no significant effect on prognosis. CC is characterized by high production of human chorionic gonadotropin (HCG) ( 24 ), probably due to the ability of CC to create placental variants ( 25 ). It has been shown ( 26 ) that high HCG levels are associated with CC secondary to the second primary tumor (SPT), whose most common SPTs are acute myeloid leukemia and thyroid cancer. It has also been shown ( 27 ) that high levels of HCG reduce the incidence of breast cancer and that women with the previous CC have a 79% lower risk of developing breast cancer compared to the general population of women. In addition, high levels of HCG primary intracranial CC predispose to tumor bleeding and progressive extracranial and cerebrospinal fluid metastases, so in the case of very high levels of HCG, we may no longer need a biopsy for histological diagnosis to avoid life-threatening tumor bleeding ( 28 ). Currently, the World Health Organization proposes the Federation International of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) score for risk assessment of gestational trophoblastic tumors. However, the prediction model constructed in this study differs from it and shows its specific advantages. First, the FIGO score is used to assess the risk of gestational trophoblastic tumors, such as GCC, whereas the prediction model developed in our study provides individualized OS assessment for CC of all origins, including both GCC and NGCC (e.g., non-gestational ovarian CC, testicular CC), which can provide guidance for NGCC in the clinic. Second, the FIGO system primarily stratifies GCC risk for treatment selection, categorizing patients into low-risk (0–6 points; single-agent chemotherapy) and high-risk (≥7 points; multiagent chemotherapy) groups ( 29 , 30 ). In contrast, our model evaluates individualized OS rates for all CC subtypes. And it is well known that the survival rates differ significantly based on individual patient characteristics, site of origin, and treatment modalities. In addition, our predictive model predicted 1-, 3-, and 5-year survival rates for different CC patients, which could not be assessed by the FIGO score. In conclusion, our prediction model demonstrated its unique ability in the prognostic assessment of CC, which is intuitive, precise and clinically useful. It can provide better value for clinical CC patients. However, the SEER database does not currently have information on specific chemotherapy regimens and definitive chemotherapy start times, which is an area for future improvement. Currently, studies show that some novel drugs (chondroitin sulfate A-binding peptides, p97/VCP inhibitors, etc.) can be used in CC patients ( 31 , 32 ). In the future, these drugs could be widely used for targeted therapy in CC patients. Serum β-HCG levels are widely used as a valid criterion for the diagnosis and therapeutic efficacy of CC. Even if serum HCG levels reach normal, consolidation chemotherapy is still recommended to prevent recurrence ( 3 , 33 , 34 ). However, for GCC patients who undergo complete tumor resection without prior chemotherapy and achieve sustained β-hCG normalization, adjuvant chemotherapy may be omitted ( 35 ). Meantime, there is a lack of data on HCG in the SEER database, so the correlation between HCG and CC was not analyzed in this study for the time being, pending the improvement of subsequent studies. Our findings demonstrate that male CC patients with coexisting germ cell tumor components exhibit a heightened propensity for pulmonary metastasis. Prognosis is significantly worse in elderly patients, those with a primary in the non-female reproductive system (e.g., male reproductive system or other organs), and individuals presenting with pulmonary or hepatic metastases. Conversely, favorable outcomes are observed in cases with surgical intervention and placental-origin CC. Notably, chemotherapy and radiotherapy showed no significant prognostic impact in this cohort. While these prognostic factors align with established clinical knowledge, we have advanced the field by constructing a nomogram prediction model to quantify the weight of each variable, enabling clinicians to develop personalized treatment plans that maximize patient benefit. There are some limitations in this study. First, due to the content of the SEER database, there were a number of relevant covariates that were not included in this study, such as metastasis timing, immunotherapy exposure, β-hCG kinetics, and comorbidities, which may have introduced selection bias. Second, absence of chemotherapy regimen classifications [e.g., EMA-CO vs. bleomycin, etoposide, cisplatin (BEP)] and radiotherapy techniques [e.g., intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT) vs. stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT)] limits therapeutic correlation analysis. Third, tumor mutation status (e.g., KRAS or TP53) and programmed death-ligand 1 (PD-L1) expression data were not captured, hindering precision oncology applications. It is encouraging to note that the SEER database is being continuously updated. We anticipate that in the future, the currently unavailable data fields (such as metastasis timing, immunotherapy exposure, β-hCG kinetics, and comorbidities) will be further supplemented, thereby enabling more comprehensive and refined analyses in related research endeavors. Conclusions This study bridges clinicopathological factors and quantitative risk stratification, offering a practical tool for CC management. Future iterations incorporating molecular biomarkers and treatment-response data could further refine predictive accuracy. Supplementary The article’s supplementary files as tcr-14-08-4867-rc.pdf (248.7KB, pdf) DOI: 10.21037/tcr-2025-206 tcr-14-08-4867-coif.pdf (323KB, pdf) DOI: 10.21037/tcr-2025-206 Acknowledgments None. Ethical Statement: The authors are accountable for all aspects of the work in ensuring that questions related to the accuracy or integrity of any part of the work are appropriately investigated and resolved. The study was conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki and its subsequent amendments. Footnotes Reporting Checklist: The authors have completed the TRIPOD reporting checklist. Available at https://tcr.amegroups.com/article/view/10.21