weight, and 9.6% were obese. Significant differences were observed among BMI groups in waist circumference, waist-to-hip ratio, body weight, and homeostasis model assessment of insulin resistance (P < 0.05). Differences were also noted in ovarian function–related indicators, including AMH, bFSH, basal luteinizing hormone (bLH), basal estradiol (bE2), and basal progesterone (bP4) levels (P < 0.05). Additionally, ovarian superovulation–related variables, such as the initiation dose of gonadotropin, the total gonadotropin dose per cycle, and the number of oocytes retrieved, varied significantly between BMI subgroups (P < 0.05). However, no significant differences were found in height, AFC, prolactin, or testosterone levels among the BMI categories. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Flow Chart of the study cohort. Table 2. Baseline characteristics of the study population. Variables BMI P value Total (n = 649) <18.5 (n = 44) 18.5-23.9 (n = 376) 24-27.9 (n = 167) ≥28 (n = 62) Age (y) 29.7 ± 3.6 28.0 ± 2.9 29.5 ± 3.5 30.2 ± 3.7 30.5 ± 4.1 < 0.001 Wt (Kg) 57.7 ± 9.1 44.3 ± 3.5 53.8 ± 4.6 63.5 ± 4.4 75.5 ± 6.9 < 0.001 Ht (cm) 158.2 ± 4.6 159.2 ± 5.1 158.2 ± 4.4 158.0 ± 4.6 157.9 ± 5.6 0.502 WC (cm) 78.1 ± 8.6 67.4 ± 5.3 75.2 ± 6.1 82.3 ± 6.5 92.7 ± 5.8 < 0.001 HC (cm) 92.7 ± 7.1 85.2 ± 5.5 90.0 ± 4.8 96.4 ± 5.0 104.9 ± 5.9 < 0.001 WHR 0.84 ± 0.06 0.79 ± 0.04 0.84 ± 0.06 0.85 ± 0.05 0.89 ± 0.06 < 0.001 AMH (ng/ml) 9.3 ± 4.7 10.7 ± 5.0 9.6 ± 4.6 9.2 ± 5.0 6.7 ± 3.5 < 0.001 AFC 24.7 ± 6.3 24.6 ± 5.6 24.5 ± 6.5 25.1 ± 6.5 25.2 ± 5.3 0.731 bFSH (IU/L) 6.3 ± 1.5 6.9 ± 1.5 6.3 ± 1.5 6.1 ± 1.5 5.8 ± 1.3 0.001 bLH (IU/L) 8.7 ± 4.8 10.9 ± 5.9 9.1 ± 4.8 8.1 ± 4.1 7.0 ± 4.3 < 0.001 bLH/bFSH 1.4 ± 0.8 1.6 ± 0.9 1.5 ± 0.9 1.3 ± 0.6 1.2 ± 0.7 0.012 bE2 (pg/ml) 41.0 ± 26.1 53.1 ± 41.6 41.1 ± 26.0 39.7 ± 22.6 35.3 ± 17.5 0.005 bP4 (ng/ml) 0.28 (0.14, 0.47) 0.38 (0.24, 0.58) 0.27 (0.14, 0.46) 0.30 (0.15, 0.49) 0.20 (0.08, 0.43) 0.004 bE2/bP4 219.3 ± 241.4 202.5 ± 221.5 219.7 ± 245.5 202.2 ± 235.1 2